    fullDescription: "MGF (Mechano Growth Factor) is the 24-amino acid C-terminal E-domain peptide derived from the IGF-1 Ec splice variant, a local muscle-specific isoform of insulin-like growth factor-1 generated in skeletal muscle through alternative RNA splicing in response to mechanical load, exercise, or tissue damage. Unlike systemic IGF-1 (primarily produced by the liver and circulating in blood), MGF is produced locally at the site of mechanical stimulus and acts in a paracrine/autocrine manner within the muscle fiber and its surrounding satellite cell niche. The unique E-domain sequence of the Ec variant distinguishes MGF functionally from liver-derived IGF-1, with a distinct receptor-binding profile that preferentially activates muscle satellite cells rather than the systemic growth effects of IGF-1.\n\nMGF activates quiescent muscle satellite cells — the Pax7-expressing resident stem cells responsible for muscle repair, regeneration, and hypertrophy — promoting their entry into the proliferative cell cycle through activation of MyoD expression and downstream muscle regulatory factor programs. This satellite cell recruitment is the initial, critical step for adaptive muscle hypertrophy following resistance training. Concurrently, the IGF-1R-mediated PI3K/Akt/mTOR signaling component of MGF drives myofibrillar protein synthesis in mature myofibers, producing combined hyperplastic (fiber number) and hypertrophic (fiber size) muscle growth responses. In cardiac muscle research, MGF activates cardiac progenitor cells and promotes cardiomyocyte survival following ischemic injury.\n\nMGF degrades rapidly in biological fluids (half-life of minutes) due to proteolytic sensitivity — a key difference from its PEGylated form. This rapid degradation makes unmodified MGF a useful research tool for studying the acute phase of satellite cell activation kinetics and for comparison with the prolonged signaling profile of PEG-MGF. Research applications include muscle satellite cell biology, mechanotransduction, IGF-1 splice variant pharmacology, exercise physiology, cachexia and sarcopenia mechanisms, and regenerative medicine research involving myogenic stem cell activation.",
