    fullDescription: "ACTH(4-10) is a heptapeptide fragment encompassing positions Met4 through Gly10 of human adrenocorticotropic hormone, representing the core 'message' sequence that drives melanocortin receptor activation. It contains the essential His-Phe-Arg-Trp (HFRW) pharmacophore at internal positions (His6-Phe7-Arg8-Trp9 in parent ACTH numbering, positions 3-6 in the fragment), which is the minimal tetrapeptide required for melanocortin receptor binding. ACTH(4-10) has been extensively studied as a neuropharmacological tool because it retains significant activity at MC4R (the primary CNS melanocortin receptor) and MC3R, contributing to cognitive enhancement, neuroprotection, and anti-inflammatory effects, while having reduced potency at the adrenal MC2R compared to full-length ACTH.\n\nA large body of neuropharmacological research from the 1970s to the present has established that ACTH(4-10) and related short melanocortin fragments produce robust effects on learning, memory consolidation, attention, and motivation in rodent models. Intracerebroventricular or intraperitoneal administration of ACTH(4-10) in rats and mice improves performance in passive and active avoidance tests, shuttle box paradigms, and spatial memory tasks. These behavioural effects occur independently of adrenal steroidogenesis (since the fragment cannot adequately stimulate MC2R), demonstrating direct CNS actions via MC4R and MC3R. The peptide has been investigated as a candidate for the treatment of peripheral neuropathy and nerve regeneration, with published reports of accelerated nerve repair following ACTH(4-10) administration in animal models.\n\nACTH(4-10) and its analogs (including Org 2766 / ACTH(4-9) and Semax / MEHFPGP) remain active areas of peptide neuropharmacology research. The fragment is used in competitive radioreceptor binding assays to define the pharmacophore requirements for melanocortin receptor subtypes, in structure-activity relationship studies comparing potencies of ACTH(4-9), (4-10), (4-11), and their modified analogs, and as a reference standard for evaluating new melanocortin drug candidates. Researchers use concentrations of 0.1-100 µM in cell-based cAMP assays and 0.1-10 mg/kg doses in rodent in vivo behavioural experiments.",
