    fullDescription: "Biotin-ACTH(1-39) is the full-length 39-residue human adrenocorticotropic hormone modified with a biotin moiety at the N-terminus. Biotin (vitamin B7, MW 244 g/mol) is attached via an aminocaproic acid or similar spacer to the Ser1 alpha-amino group, providing a high-affinity capture handle (Kd ~10⁻¹⁵ M for streptavidin binding) without sterically blocking the C-terminal address domain required for MC2R interaction. The overall molecular weight is approximately 4783 g/mol (ACTH 1-39 MW 4541 + biotin group ~244 g/mol). This conjugate retains biological activity at melanocortin receptors, although N-terminal biotinylation may modestly affect MC2R binding affinity — a consideration that should be evaluated empirically for specific experimental applications.\n\nBiotin-ACTH(1-39) is used primarily in biochemical assays requiring physical capture or immobilisation of the ACTH molecule. In pull-down experiments, Biotin-ACTH(1-39) is incubated with cell lysates, membrane extracts, or purified MC2R/MC4R preparations, and the biotin handle allows quantitative recovery of ACTH-binding proteins using streptavidin-coated magnetic beads or agarose. This approach enables identification and characterisation of novel ACTH-interacting proteins beyond the classical MC2R/MC4R receptors, including putative ACTH-binding accessory proteins and plasma carrier proteins. In surface plasmon resonance (SPR) and biolayer interferometry (BLI) assays, Biotin-ACTH(1-39) can be immobilised on streptavidin-coated sensor chips for real-time kinetic binding measurements with recombinant melanocortin receptor ectodomains.\n\nIn ELISA and antibody development applications, Biotin-ACTH(1-39) serves as the capture or detection agent in sandwich immunoassay formats. By coating streptavidin-ELISA plates with Biotin-ACTH(1-39), researchers can screen hybridoma supernatants or phage display libraries for ACTH-binding antibodies with defined epitope specificity. The biotinylated peptide is also used in proximity ligation assays (PLA) and FRET-based binding studies to monitor ACTH-receptor interactions at the cell surface without requiring radioactive isotopes. For researchers studying ACTH processing and POMC post-translational modifications, Biotin-ACTH(1-39) serves as a reference standard in streptavidin-HRP-based western blotting procedures.",
