    fullDescription: "Human corticostatin is a member of the α-defensin family of host-defence peptides, closely related to human neutrophil peptides HNP-1, HNP-2, and HNP-3. These 29-34 residue cationic peptides are stored in the primary (azurophil) granules of human neutrophils and are released into phagolysosomes upon neutrophil activation or into the extracellular space during infection. The characteristic triple-stranded β-sheet structure is stabilised by three conserved intramolecular disulfide bonds (Cys2-Cys30, Cys4-Cys19, and Cys9-Cys29 in HNP-1 numbering), forming the 'defensin fold' that confers resistance to protease degradation and enables membrane interaction. The term 'corticostatin' was applied to this peptide family following the discovery that these neutrophil-derived defensins potently inhibit ACTH-stimulated cortisol secretion in rat adrenocortical cells, providing a molecular link between innate immunity and HPA axis function.\n\nThe corticostatin designation reflects the peptide's ability to antagonise MC2R signaling in adrenocortical cells. At nanomolar concentrations, corticostatin (HNP-1/HNP-2 preparations) inhibit ACTH-stimulated cAMP accumulation and cortisol synthesis in isolated rat adrenal cells, demonstrating competitive antagonism or functional blocking of the ACTH receptor. This anti-cortisol property was proposed to represent an immunological mechanism by which activated neutrophils can modulate adrenal corticosteroid output during infection and sepsis, potentially preventing excessive immunosuppression by glucocorticoids at sites of acute inflammation. The corticostatin activity is independent of its antimicrobial function and requires the intact defensin fold with functional disulfide bonds.\n\nBeyond its corticostatin/adrenal activity, human HNP-1/corticostatin exhibits potent direct antimicrobial activity against gram-positive and gram-negative bacteria, fungi, and enveloped viruses including HIV-1. The antimicrobial mechanism involves electrostatic attraction to negatively charged bacterial membranes followed by membrane disruption via pore formation or membrane thinning. Corticostatin has been shown to inhibit HIV-1 infection by binding gp120 and blocking viral attachment to host CD4+ T cells and macrophages. Researchers use synthetic corticostatin/HNP-1 to study these multiple activities, to define structure-activity relationships of defensin-class peptides, and to develop antimicrobial peptide therapeutics for multidrug-resistant bacterial infections.",
