    fullDescription: "[Phe2,Nle4]-ACTH(1-24) is a synthetic analog of cosyntropin (ACTH(1-24)) containing two strategically placed amino acid substitutions: Tyr2→Phe and Met4→Nle (norleucine). The Tyr2→Phe substitution replaces the hydroxyl-bearing aromatic Tyr at position 2 with Phe, removing the phenol hydroxyl group and its potential for tyrosine-specific enzymatic modifications. The Met4→Nle substitution replaces the sulfur-containing Met with the isosteric norleucine (a straight-chain C6 amino acid), eliminating the Met sulfur that is susceptible to oxidation under oxidative stress conditions or during radioiodination procedures using ¹²⁵I and chloramine-T. Together, these substitutions produce an ACTH(1-24) analog with substantially improved chemical stability relative to the parent cosyntropin, while retaining comparable or enhanced biological potency at melanocortin receptors.\n\nThe primary application of [Phe2,Nle4]-ACTH(1-24) is as a metabolically and chemically stable ACTH analog for radioiodination studies. When cosyntropin (ACTH(1-24)) is radioiodinated using ¹²⁵I, the process inevitably labels both Tyr2 and Tyr23, producing multiple radiolabelled species with variable receptor-binding properties. By replacing Tyr2 with Phe, [Phe2,Nle4]-ACTH(1-24) allows selective monoiodination at Tyr23 alone, yielding a structurally homogeneous radioligand with defined specific activity and reproducible receptor-binding pharmacology. The Nle4 substitution simultaneously prevents Met4 oxidation during the iodination procedure, further improving batch-to-batch consistency. The resulting radiolabelled [¹²⁵I-Tyr23,Phe2,Nle4]-ACTH(1-24) is a gold-standard radioligand for quantitative MC2R and MC4R binding studies.\n\nBeyond radioligand applications, [Phe2,Nle4]-ACTH(1-24) is used as an oxidation-stable reference standard in biological assays where Met oxidation in standard cosyntropin would introduce variability. In assays measuring adrenocortical cAMP production, steroidogenesis, or MC4R-mediated signaling in neuronal cells, the double-substituted analog provides consistent dose-response curves unaffected by Met oxidation. Structure-activity studies comparing [Phe2,Nle4]-ACTH(1-24) with [Nle4]-ACTH(1-24), [Phe2]-ACTH(1-24), and unmodified ACTH(1-24) have established the independent and combined contributions of positions 2 and 4 to receptor affinity and selectivity across the five melanocortin receptor subtypes.",
