    shortDescription:
      "Human neutrophil peptide 2 (HNP-2), an α-defensin with broad-spectrum antimicrobial and immunomodulatory activity. Used in host-defence, innate immunity, and antimicrobial peptide research.",
    fullDescription: "Human neutrophil peptide 2 (HNP-2), also designated alpha-defensin 2 (DEFA2), is a 29-amino acid cationic β-sheet peptide from the α-defensin family stored in the primary (azurophil) granules of human neutrophils. HNP-2 is the N-terminally truncated form of HNP-1 (which begins with Ala-Cys-Tyr...) and shares the identical sequence from position 2 onwards, beginning instead with Cys-Tyr-Cys. All four human neutrophil peptides (HNP-1 through HNP-4) are post-translationally processed from a larger precursor and are co-stored in neutrophil granules at combined concentrations reaching milligrams per milliliter, representing a substantial antimicrobial arsenal deployed upon neutrophil degranulation at infection sites. The three intramolecular disulfide bonds of HNP-2 (connecting Cys1-Cys29, Cys3-Cys28, and Cys8-Cys18 in canonical connectivity) form the 'defensin-fold' triple-stranded antiparallel β-sheet stabilised by a 'tridisulfide' motif that confers extraordinary protease resistance under host and pathogen protease conditions.\n\nHNP-2's antimicrobial mechanism involves electrostatic attraction to the negatively charged bacterial surface (lipopolysaccharide in gram-negative bacteria; lipoteichoic acid and cell wall in gram-positive bacteria) followed by membrane permeabilisation through a combination of pore formation and lipid bilayer disruption. The peptide shows potent bactericidal activity against Staphylococcus aureus, Escherichia coli, Pseudomonas aeruginosa, and Klebsiella pneumoniae at micromolar concentrations, including methicillin-resistant S. aureus (MRSA) strains resistant to conventional antibiotics. HNP-2 also inhibits enveloped viruses including HIV-1 (by disrupting the viral envelope and by blocking gp120-CD4 receptor binding), herpes simplex virus types 1 and 2, and influenza A virus, demonstrating breadth of direct antiviral activity independent of its immunomodulatory functions.\n\nBeyond direct antimicrobial activity, HNP-2 and other α-defensins function as immunomodulatory cytokine-like agents. HNP-2 induces the production of pro-inflammatory cytokines (IL-1β, TNF-α, IL-6) from macrophages, acts as a chemoattractant for immature dendritic cells and memory T cells, activates the complement system, and modulates mast cell degranulation. These immunostimulatory properties position HNP-2 at the interface of innate and adaptive immunity, acting as an alarm signal that amplifies immune responses at sites of infection or tissue damage. Researchers use synthetic HNP-2 in studies of antimicrobial peptide (AMP) mechanism of action, structure-activity relationships for the defensin scaffold, and innate immune signaling pathway characterisation.",
