    fullDescription: "Allatostatin I (AST-1) is the prototypical member of the type-A allatostatin (cockroach-type allatostatin) family, originally isolated from the Pacific beetle cockroach Diploptera punctata but structurally conserved across numerous insect orders. It is a 13-residue C-terminally amidated neuropeptide bearing the characteristic -YXFGLamide motif (Tyr-Gly-Phe-Gly-Leu-NH₂ at positions 9-13) that defines the type-A allatostatin family. Allatostatin I is biosynthesised as a polyprotein precursor in neurosecretory cells of the brain and processed to release multiple active allatostatin peptides that act on the corpus allatum — the endocrine gland responsible for juvenile hormone (JH) biosynthesis in insects. JH is a sesquiterpenoid hormone that controls insect developmental transitions, reproduction, and metamorphosis, making allatostatins key regulators of insect life history.\n\nThe primary biological action of Allatostatin I is inhibition of JH biosynthesis in corpus allatum tissue, achieved through binding to G protein-coupled allatostatin receptors (AlstR family) on corpus allatum cells, reducing intracellular cAMP and inhibiting HMG-CoA reductase activity required for isoprenoid (JH) synthesis. Allatostatin I is widely used in experimental invertebrate neuroendocrinology to pharmacologically suppress JH biosynthesis in isolated corpus allata preparations from cockroaches, locusts, and crickets, enabling researchers to study the consequences of JH deficiency on moulting, reproduction, and metamorphosis. In transgenic Drosophila melanogaster systems, the AlstR receptor has been expressed in specific neuron populations to enable synthetic genetic silencing of neuron activity upon Allatostatin I application, providing a powerful tool analogous to the DREADD (Designer Receptors Exclusively Activated by Designer Drugs) approach for mapping insect neural circuits.\n\nAllatostatin I and its congeners (Allatostatin II, III, IV, etc.) are foundational research tools in insect physiology and chemical biology. They are used in studies of allatostatin receptor structure-activity relationships, the regulation of insect reproductive diapause, and the development of novel insect growth regulators (IGRs) as potential pest management agents targeting JH biosynthesis. The peptide is reconstituted in aqueous buffer (PBS or 0.1% BSA in water) and applied to corpus allatum preparations at nanomolar concentrations for ex vivo JH biosynthesis assays, or applied to live insects at picomolar-nanomolar doses via injection for in vivo reproductive and developmental studies.",
