    fullDescription: "Teprotide (SQ 20,881) is a nonapeptide ACE inhibitor originally isolated from the venom of the Brazilian pit viper Bothrops jararaca, where it was identified as one of several bradykinin potentiating peptides (BPPs) that suppress angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) activity and potentiate the hypotensive effects of bradykinin. Teprotide is the compound that directly inspired the development of the first orally active ACE inhibitor drug — captopril — by David Cushman and Miguel Ondetti at Squibb in the late 1970s. Structurally, teprotide is characterised by a pyroglutamate N-terminus (protecting against aminopeptidase degradation, as seen in AKH peptides and TRH), a proline-rich sequence (Pro appears at positions 3, 5, and 8-9), and a C-terminal proline — features that mimic the natural bradykinin C-terminal Pro-Pro motif that prevents ACE-mediated bradykinin degradation while simultaneously occupying the ACE active site.\n\nTeprotide inhibits ACE (also known as kininase II) by coordinating to the catalytic zinc ion in the ACE active site through its C-terminal proline carboxylate and by engaging subsites S1, S2, and S2' of the enzyme's catalytic cleft. By inhibiting ACE, teprotide blocks both the conversion of angiotensin I to the vasoconstrictor angiotensin II and the degradation of the vasodilator bradykinin, producing a dual antihypertensive effect. Early clinical studies in the 1970s demonstrated that intravenous teprotide dramatically reduced blood pressure in patients with hypertension, particularly renin-dependent hypertension, validating the RAAS as a pharmacological target and establishing ACE inhibition as an antihypertensive mechanism. The success of teprotide in clinical proof-of-concept studies directly motivated the development of orally bioavailable, smaller-molecule ACE inhibitors.\n\nSynthetic teprotide is used in contemporary research as a reference ACE inhibitor in enzyme activity assays, as a positive control for ACE inhibition studies using fluorogenic substrates (e.g., hippuryl-His-Leu, MCA-RPPGFSAFK), and in comparative pharmacology studies evaluating the ACE inhibitory potency of novel peptides derived from food protein hydrolysates (milk casein, soy protein, fish collagen) and natural product extracts. Teprotide's Ki for ACE inhibition is in the low nanomolar range (approximately 1-3 nM), serving as a benchmark for new ACE inhibitory peptide candidates. It is also used as a historical reference compound in pharmacological education and in translational studies tracing the evolution of peptide drug discovery from snake venom biochemistry to modern cardiovascular therapeutics.",
