    fullDescription: "Bradykinin potentiating peptide C (BPP-c) is a 10-residue ACE inhibitory peptide from the venom of Bothrops jararaca, representing a longer member of the BPP venom peptide family compared to BPP-b (7-mer) and positioning between the shorter BPPs and the teprotide nonapeptide in the BPP size series. BPP-c shares the common family characteristics — N-terminal pyroglutamate, proline-rich sequence, C-terminal Pro for ACE active site coordination — but its specific sequence (with Pro at positions 2, 3, 7, and 10) and the acidic Asp residue at position 8 give it a distinct pharmacological profile that has been explored in studies examining how different BPP sequences interact with the two ACE active sites (C-domain and N-domain) of the somatic ACE enzyme. Selective inhibition of ACE C-domain vs. N-domain active sites has emerged as a pharmacologically significant distinction relevant to side-effect profiles of ACE inhibitor drugs.\n\nLike other BPPs, BPP-c was discovered in studies by Sérgio Ferreira's group in the 1960s-70s characterising the hypotensive peptides in Bothrops jararaca venom that potentiated the hypotensive action of bradykinin. BPP-c inhibits ACE by binding the active site zinc with its C-terminal Pro and positioning along the substrate binding groove. Its 10-residue length enables additional contacts with ACE surface residues compared to shorter BPP-b, contributing to higher ACE inhibitory affinity in some assays. BPP-c has been reported to have additional pharmacological activity beyond ACE inhibition: several BPPs have been found to directly modulate endothelin A and B receptors, potentiate bradykinin activity at the B2 receptor independently of ACE inhibition, and activate neutral endopeptidase, indicating that these snake venom peptides have pleiotropic cardiovascular pharmacology.\n\nSynthetic BPP-c is used in comparative pharmacology studies of the BPP family alongside BPP-b and teprotide, enabling mapping of ACE inhibitor potency as a function of chain length, Pro density, and specific residue composition within the BPP family. It is employed in fluorometric ACE inhibition assays using hippuryl-His-Leu or MCA-peptide substrates, in ACE domain selectivity studies (C-domain vs. N-domain ACE), and as a historical reference compound in the study of snake venom cardiovascular pharmacology. BPP-c is reconstituted in water or PBS and tested at nanomolar to micromolar concentrations in enzymatic assays and bradykinin potentiation bioassays.",
