    fullDescription: "Angiotensin I converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitor peptide is a short research peptide used in mechanistic studies of ACE inhibition and antihypertensive peptide pharmacology. ACE (also known as kininase II, EC 3.4.15.1) is a zinc metallopeptidase that catalyses the conversion of the inactive decapeptide angiotensin I to the potent vasoconstrictor angiotensin II by removing the C-terminal His-Leu dipeptide, and also inactivates the vasodilator bradykinin. Inhibition of ACE is a well-established strategy for treating hypertension, heart failure, and diabetic nephropathy, forming the mechanistic basis of the ACE inhibitor drug class (captopril, enalapril, lisinopril, etc.). Research-grade ACE inhibitor peptides are used as reference standards in fluorometric and colorimetric ACE inhibition assays, providing IC50 and Ki data for comparative pharmacology studies.\n\nFood-derived ACE inhibitory peptides (from milk, soy, fish, egg, and plant protein hydrolysates) represent an active area of nutraceutical research where tripeptides and dipeptides with C-terminal Pro or hydrophobic residues show antihypertensive activity in vitro and in spontaneously hypertensive rat (SHR) models. Milk casein hydrolysis-derived tripeptides such as Val-Pro-Pro (VPP) and Ile-Pro-Pro (IPP) have entered functional food products in Japan and Europe for blood pressure management. Short ACE inhibitor peptides like Ala-Pro-Pro provide mechanistic data for understanding how the ACE active site S2' subsite preference for Pro at the penultimate C-terminal position drives the pharmacophore requirements for ACE inhibitor peptide design.\n\nIn biochemical assays, ACE inhibitor peptides are applied at micromolar concentrations to ACE preparations (porcine kidney or human recombinant) with fluorogenic substrates to determine IC50 values. Animal studies using spontaneously hypertensive rats (SHR) employ oral administration of ACE inhibitor peptides at milligram-per-kilogram doses to evaluate antihypertensive effects in vivo, complementing in vitro enzyme kinetics with systolic blood pressure measurements. Researchers use panels of ACE inhibitor peptides with defined sequences to map the ACE S1-S2-S1'-S2' subsite preferences that govern peptide inhibitor design, and to investigate the dual-domain (N-domain and C-domain) selectivity of novel ACE inhibitor peptides as potential antihypertensive leads.",
