    fullDescription: "Mca-YVADAPK(Dnp) is a FRET (Förster Resonance Energy Transfer)-based fluorogenic substrate specifically designed for continuous, real-time measurement of caspase-1 (ICE, interleukin-1β converting enzyme) protease activity. The substrate contains two key elements: a 7-methoxycoumarin-4-acetic acid (Mca) fluorophore at the N-terminus that fluoresces at approximately 393 nm upon excitation at 328 nm, and a 2,4-dinitrophenyl (Dnp) quencher attached to the ε-amine of Lys at position 7. In the intact substrate, FRET energy transfer from excited Mca to the nearby Dnp quencher efficiently suppresses Mca fluorescence. The YVAD tetrapeptide sequence (Tyr-Val-Ala-Asp) at positions 2-5 constitutes the optimal caspase-1 recognition sequence (derived from the caspase-1 natural substrate pro-IL-1β at the Asp116 cleavage site), and cleavage at the Asp↓Ala bond separates the Mca fluorophore from the Dnp quencher, producing a linear increase in fluorescence signal directly proportional to caspase-1 activity.\n\nCaspase-1 is the proteolytic enzyme at the core of the NLRP3, NLRP1, and NLRC4 inflammasome complexes — supramolecular platforms assembled in macrophages and other innate immune cells in response to danger-associated molecular patterns (DAMPs) and pathogen-associated molecular patterns (PAMPs). Active caspase-1 cleaves pro-interleukin-1β (pro-IL-1β) and pro-IL-18 to generate the mature cytokines, and also cleaves gasdermin-D (GSDMD) to trigger pyroptosis — a lytic, pro-inflammatory form of cell death. Mca-YVADAPK(Dnp) enables continuous kinetic measurement of caspase-1 activity in inflammasome-stimulated cell lysates (LPS + nigericin, LPS + ATP, or silica-stimulated macrophages), making it a standard tool for quantifying inflammasome activation and for screening caspase-1 inhibitor candidates.\n\nThe YVAD recognition sequence provides selectivity for caspase-1 over the apoptotic executioner caspases caspase-3 and caspase-7 (which preferentially cleave DEVD sequences), allowing differential measurement of inflammasome-associated vs. apoptotic caspase activity in the same samples using orthogonal substrates with different excitation/emission wavelengths. Mca-YVADAPK(Dnp) is dissolved in DMSO (typically 10 mM stock) and diluted to 10-50 µM in assay buffer for enzymatic reactions. It is routinely used in drug discovery screens for NLRP3 inflammasome inhibitors, in studies characterising the kinetics of caspase-1 activation in response to sterile inflammatory signals, and in mechanistic studies of pyroptosis induction and regulation.",
