    fullDescription: "Matrixyl Acetate is the acetate salt form of Palmitoyl Pentapeptide-4 (INCI: Palmitoyl Pentapeptide-4), also known commercially as Matrixyl® (Sederma/Croda). The active component is the lipophilic peptide Pal-Lys-Thr-Thr-Lys-Ser (Palmitoyl-KTTKS), derived from the KTTKS sequence found within the fibronectin type III domain (FN-III repeat) of type I procollagen. The palmitoyl chain (C16 fatty acid) enhances transdermal penetration by increasing lipophilicity, facilitating passage through the stratum corneum lipid matrix to reach the viable epidermis and upper dermis where dermal fibroblasts are located. Once in the dermis, the peptide core (KTTKS) acts as a messenger signal to activate fibroblast collagen and extracellular matrix (ECM) production pathways, mimicking the endogenous matrix-regulatory signals that decline with skin aging.\n\nThe mechanism of Palmitoyl Pentapeptide-4 involves binding to cell surface receptors (proposed to involve TGF-β receptor complexes and cell adhesion receptors that recognise fibronectin-derived signals), stimulating the production of collagen I, collagen III, collagen IV, fibronectin, and hyaluronic acid in dermal fibroblasts. Multiple in vitro studies using human fibroblast cultures have demonstrated dose-dependent increases in procollagen mRNA expression and collagen protein secretion at concentrations of 1-100 nM, consistent with a receptor-mediated agonist mechanism rather than simple membrane perturbation. In vivo double-blind clinical trials in human volunteers have reported improvements in skin texture, wrinkle depth, and dermal density after 4-8 weeks of topical application with formulations containing 3-5 µM Matrixyl.\n\nMatrixyl Acetate is used in cosmetic research laboratories to investigate the molecular mechanisms of ECM remodelling stimulation by lipopeptides, the role of specific receptor pathways (TGF-β, fibronectin receptor, integrin-mediated signaling) in fibroblast activation, and the structure-activity relationships within the KTTKS peptide series. It serves as the gold-standard anti-aging lipopeptide reference against which newer cosmetic peptides (such as Matrixyl 3000 components and other palmitoyl peptides) are compared in fibroblast collagen synthesis assays. Research applications include studying the dose-response kinetics of collagen stimulation, examining synergies with retinoids and growth factors, and characterising the metabolic fate of lipopeptides in skin model systems.",
