    fullDescription: "Tegopep (Tegopep-6-L, Lauroyl Ile-Gly-Ile) is a cosmetic lauroyl tripeptide developed by Evonik Industries (formerly Goldschmidt) as a multifunctional skin and hair conditioning ingredient. The compound consists of a lauric acid (C12 fatty acid) chain attached via an amide bond to the tripeptide Ile-Gly-Ile, combining the moisturising and film-forming properties of a fatty acid with the amino acid-based affinity for keratin proteins found in skin and hair. The lauroyl lipid component contributes to skin barrier integration and provides emollient properties, while the Ile-Gly-Ile peptide sequence has affinity for keratinous substrates, promoting adsorption to skin and hair surfaces. Tegopep's amphiphilic structure enables it to self-assemble into lamellar structures at the skin-air interface, contributing to barrier function enhancement.\n\nIn skin research applications, Tegopep is studied for its ability to improve stratum corneum hydration by forming moisture-retaining lipopeptide films on the corneocyte surface, supplementing the natural moisturising factor (NMF) and reducing transepidermal water loss (TEWL). In vitro studies on reconstructed human skin models have shown that formulations containing Tegopep maintain higher hydration levels compared to vehicle controls, and cell culture experiments suggest modulation of aquaporin-3 expression in keratinocytes — a water channel protein involved in keratinocyte hydration and differentiation. In hair conditioning research, Tegopep demonstrates substantivity to chemically damaged (bleached or permed) hair fibers, improving wet combing forces and surface smoothness as measured by friction coefficient and SEM morphology analysis.\n\nTegopep is incorporated into skin care (moisturisers, serums, face creams) and hair care (conditioners, treatment masks) formulations at 0.1-3% (w/w) concentrations and is compatible with a wide range of cosmetic emulsion systems, surfactant formulations, and aqueous serum systems due to its moderate HLB value. Research applications include comparative conditioning efficacy studies against conventional silicone-based conditioners and other lauroyl amino acid derivatives, investigation of lipopeptide-keratin adsorption kinetics by quartz crystal microbalance (QCM), and skin barrier function studies using in vitro TEWL measurements and confocal Raman spectroscopy for stratum corneum depth profiling. Tegopep's dual skin/hair functionality makes it a versatile platform ingredient for multibenefit personal care formulation development.",
