    fullDescription: "L-Carnosine (β-alanyl-L-histidine) is a naturally occurring dipeptide found at high concentrations in skeletal muscle (up to 20 mM) and brain tissue, where it was first isolated by Gulewitsch and Amiradzibi in 1900. Its unique structure — combining a non-proteogenic β-amino acid (β-alanine) with the imidazole-containing amino acid histidine — endows carnosine with exceptional chemical versatility. The imidazole ring of the histidine residue has a pKa of approximately 6.83, making carnosine an efficient physiological buffer in contracting muscle during exercise-induced acidosis. Carnosine additionally chelates divalent transition metals (particularly Cu²⁺ and Zn²⁺), forming stable complexes that reduce redox-active metal-catalyzed oxidative damage to proteins and lipids. The carnosine-copper(II) complex (Cu-carnosine) itself exhibits superoxide dismutase-like activity, further contributing to its antioxidant profile.\n\nThe anti-glycation mechanism of L-Carnosine has attracted substantial research attention in the context of aging and metabolic disease. Advanced glycation end-products (AGEs) form when reducing sugars react with the free amino groups of proteins, lipids, and nucleic acids through the Maillard reaction. Carnosine acts as a competitive substrate for this reaction — its free amino terminus reacts preferentially with glycating carbonyl species (including methylglyoxal and 3-deoxyglucosone), sacrificing itself to protect long-lived structural proteins such as collagen, lens crystallins, and neuronal proteins from carbonyl stress. This carbonyl scavenging activity has been documented extensively in vitro and in animal models, with carnosine treatment reducing AGE accumulation in tissues including the kidney, eye lens, and brain vasculature. Carnosine also activates the proteasome-mediated degradation of glycated proteins, partially restoring proteostasis in aged or diabetic cells.\n\nResearch applications for L-Carnosine span longevity biology, sports science, and neuroprotection. In cellular senescence studies, carnosine extends the Hayflick limit (replicative lifespan) of cultured human fibroblasts and reverses the senescent morphology of late-passage cells upon treatment. In exercise physiology, supplementation studies demonstrate improved high-intensity exercise capacity attributed to enhanced intramuscular buffering. In neurological research, carnosine is investigated as a neuroprotective agent in models of Alzheimer's disease (where it inhibits beta-amyloid aggregation and reduces tau phosphorylation), Parkinson's disease, and ischemia-reperfusion injury. Its ability to cross the blood-brain barrier (aided by PEPT2 transporter-mediated uptake) positions carnosine as a research tool for studying neuroprotective mechanisms in age-related neurodegenerative conditions.",
