    shortDescription: "A biomimetic hair growth peptide signaling molecule. Studied for stimulating extracellular matrix proteins and follicular cell proliferation in alopecia research.",
    fullDescription: "Acetyl Tetrapeptide-3 (INCI name) is an N-acetylated tetrapeptide with the sequence Ac-Lys-Arg-Asn-Lys, designed as a biomimetic of the biologically active domain of follistatin — an extracellular matrix glycoprotein that normally antagonizes myostatin and activin signaling in hair follicles. The hair follicle unit, specifically the dermal papilla, is critically dependent on ECM integrity: the basement membrane surrounding the follicle is composed primarily of collagen IV and laminin-511/laminin-332, which form anchoring structures essential for keratinocyte attachment, follicle morphogenesis, and the cyclic progression through anagen (growth), catagen (regression), and telogen (rest) phases. In androgenetic alopecia and other hair loss conditions, degradation of these ECM anchorage proteins is a key pathophysiological event that shortens anagen duration and miniaturizes follicles.\n\nThe mechanism of Acetyl Tetrapeptide-3 centers on stimulation of ECM protein synthesis in dermal papilla fibroblasts, mimicking the follistatin-derived matrikine signal. In vitro studies demonstrate upregulation of laminin-5 (laminin-332) and collagen IV gene expression in dermal papilla cells treated with the peptide, reconstructing the basement membrane environment required for follicle anchoring and keratinocyte proliferation. Secondary effects include enhancement of fibronectin expression and downregulation of matrix metalloproteinase (MMP) activity, collectively shifting the ECM toward a pro-anagen remodeling state. The peptide also modulates the Wnt/β-catenin pathway in dermal papilla cells — a critical signaling axis governing follicle cycling — promoting nuclear β-catenin accumulation that drives anagen-phase gene expression programs.\n\nResearch applications for Acetyl Tetrapeptide-3 include in vitro alopecia model studies using dermal papilla cell cultures, investigation of ECM-follicle interaction in hair cycling, and evaluation as a cosmeceutical active ingredient for androgenetic alopecia. Clinical studies combining Acetyl Tetrapeptide-3 with Biotinoyl Tripeptide-1 have demonstrated synergistic effects on hair density metrics — particularly an increase in anagen-phase hair count and a reduction in telogen-phase shedding — in subjects with thinning hair. The compound is used in follicle biology research as a non-hormonal, ECM-targeted probe for studying the molecular determinants of anagen maintenance.",
