    fullDescription: "Carfilzomib (trade name Kyprolis®) is an irreversible tetrapeptide epoxyketone proteasome inhibitor developed by Onyx Pharmaceuticals and approved by the FDA for the treatment of relapsed or refractory multiple myeloma. Its mechanism of action is based on the selective covalent modification of the catalytic threonine residue (Thr1) at the beta-5 (chymotrypsin-like) active site of the 20S proteasome — the proteolytic core particle of the 26S proteasome complex responsible for degrading the majority of intracellular ubiquitinated proteins. By permanently inactivating the proteasome, carfilzomib prevents the degradation of pro-apoptotic proteins, disrupts NFκB signaling (which depends on IκB degradation), and induces endoplasmic reticulum stress, collectively driving myeloma cells into apoptosis while sparing most normal cells due to their higher proteasomal reserve capacity. Carfilzomib intermediate-1 represents an early-stage protected peptide precursor in the multi-step synthesis of carfilzomib.\n\nIn pharmaceutical chemistry research, carfilzomib synthesis intermediates serve multiple roles: as reference standards for process analytical chemistry, as impurity standards for regulatory filing of the active pharmaceutical ingredient (API), and as tools for investigating synthetic route optimization, stereochemical control of the epoxyketone pharmacophore, and structure-activity relationships around the peptide backbone. The synthesis of carfilzomib typically involves convergent assembly of amino acid-derived fragments followed by epoxidation of the terminal vinyl group to install the reactive warhead. This intermediate is used by researchers studying 20S proteasome inhibitor development, cancer pharmacology, and synthetic methodology for epoxyketone-based compounds.",
