    fullDescription: "Fmoc-Aib-Aib-OH is a protected dipeptide building block composed of two residues of α-aminoisobutyric acid (Aib, α-methylalanine, 2-methylalanine) with an N-terminal Fmoc protecting group for use in Fmoc/tBu solid-phase peptide synthesis. Aib (2-aminoisobutyric acid) is a non-proteinogenic, achiral alpha,alpha-disubstituted amino acid bearing two methyl groups on the alpha carbon, which severely restricts its backbone dihedral angles to the helical (α or 3₁₀ helix) conformation due to steric clash of the geminal methyls with neighboring residues. This conformational constraint makes Aib one of the most powerful helix-inducing residues known — even short runs of 2–3 consecutive Aib residues impose a stable helical backbone geometry on peptide chains in solution. The building block appears naturally in peptaibol antibiotics (e.g., alamethicin, trichovirin) produced by Trichoderma fungi, where Aib-rich sequences adopt membrane-spanning 3₁₀ helices and form ion channels.\n\nIn peptide research, Fmoc-Aib-Aib-OH is used to introduce double Aib motifs into synthetic peptides for the purpose of stabilizing helical secondary structure, studying peptide folding and helix nucleation, and designing conformationally constrained analogues of bioactive helical peptides (e.g., GLP-1, GIP, calcitonin, and parathyroid hormone). Pre-formed Aib-Aib dipeptide building blocks are preferred over sequential single Aib couplings because double Aib sequences couple poorly in automated synthesis — the combination of steric hindrance and the requirement for specialized coupling reagents makes dipeptide format more reliable. Research groups use this compound in the design of peptaibol analogues, antimicrobial peptides, stapled peptide mimetics, and engineered GLP-1 receptor agonists with improved metabolic stability.",
