    fullDescription: "GHK (Gly-His-Lys) is a naturally occurring human tripeptide first isolated from human plasma albumin by Loren Pickart in 1973. It is found in plasma (200 ng/mL in young adults, declining with age), urine, and saliva, and is produced locally during tissue remodeling through the action of proteases on specific plasma proteins including albumin. The free tripeptide has remarkably high affinity for copper(II) ions (Kd ~10⁻¹⁴ M), forming the GHK-Cu complex in which copper is coordinated by the imidazole nitrogen of histidine, the alpha-amine of glycine, and the deprotonated amide nitrogen of the Gly-His bond in a square planar geometry. This copper-binding capacity is central to many of GHK's biological activities, as copper is an essential cofactor for lysyl oxidase (collagen crosslinking), superoxide dismutase (antioxidant defense), and cytochrome c oxidase (mitochondrial energy production). The acetate salt form provides improved stability and aqueous solubility compared to other counterion formulations.\n\nGHK is one of the most extensively studied peptides in wound healing and anti-aging research. At nanomolar to micromolar concentrations, GHK-Cu stimulates dermal fibroblasts to upregulate synthesis of collagen types I and III, elastin, laminin, and fibronectin — the structural proteins of the extracellular matrix that decline with chronological and photo-aging. Mechanistically, GHK activates the TGF-β1 signaling pathway and suppresses MMP-1 (collagenase) and MMP-2/MMP-9 (gelatinase) expression, simultaneously driving ECM synthesis and inhibiting degradation. In wound healing models, GHK-Cu accelerates the migration and proliferation of keratinocytes and fibroblasts into the wound bed, stimulates angiogenesis, and reduces inflammation by downregulating TNF-α and IL-1β. Gene expression profiling studies (Pickart et al.) have documented that GHK modulates expression of over 4,000 human genes, including VEGF, HIF-1α, and genes involved in DNA repair and stem cell maintenance.\n\nAnti-aging research applications of GHK span dermatology, hair biology, neurological research, and systemic longevity biology. In skin research, topical GHK-Cu formulations have been shown in controlled clinical studies to reduce fine lines and wrinkles, improve skin elasticity and density, stimulate hair follicle proliferation, and accelerate cutaneous wound closure. Emerging neurological research investigates GHK as a neuroprotective agent, with data suggesting modulation of nerve growth factor (NGF) expression and protection against oxidative stress in neuronal cells. Studies in invertebrate and rodent aging models indicate GHK can extend lifespan and improve health span metrics, positioning it as an active area of longevity research. The acetate salt presented here is the preferred research formulation for in vitro cell culture studies and topical formulation development.",
