    fullDescription: "Corticotropin-releasing factor (CRF, also called CRH — corticotropin-releasing hormone) is a 41-amino acid neuropeptide produced primarily in the hypothalamic paraventricular nucleus (PVN) that serves as the master regulator of the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis stress response. First characterized by Vale et al. in 1981 from ovine hypothalami, human CRF shares 83% sequence identity with ovine CRF and is synthesized from a 196-amino acid precursor protein. Upon physiological or psychological stress, CRF is released from PVN terminals into the hypophyseal portal circulation, where it acts on CRF receptor type 1 (CRFR1, a class B GPCR) on anterior pituitary corticotrophs to stimulate ACTH synthesis and secretion through Gs/cAMP/PKA signaling and POMC gene transcription.\n\nCRF also functions as a broad neuromodulator throughout the CNS and peripheral tissues. In the central nervous system, CRF neurons in the amygdala, bed nucleus of the stria terminalis (BNST), and locus coeruleus mediate emotional responses to stress including fear, anxiety, and autonomic arousal. In the GI tract, CRF and urocortins (structurally related peptides) acting at CRFR1 and CRFR2 regulate gut motility, mucosal barrier function, and stress-induced colonic hypersensitivity relevant to irritable bowel syndrome (IBS). Peripheral CRF also modulates immune function, skin barrier integrity, and reproductive physiology through local autocrine/paracrine signaling.\n\nResearch applications of CRF include HPA axis studies (ACTH/cortisol dynamics, circadian stress rhythms, glucocorticoid feedback), anxiety and depression models (CRF-induced anxiogenesis, CRFR1 antagonist development), stress-related GI disorders, Cushing's disease mechanisms (pituitary CRFRsignaling in corticotroph adenomas), and translational research on stress resilience and post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD). CRF is used in functional CRF stimulation tests to distinguish pituitary from ectopic ACTH-producing tumors in the differential diagnosis of Cushing's syndrome.",
