    fullDescription: "Deslorelin is a synthetic GnRH (gonadotropin-releasing hormone) superagonist decapeptide modified at position 6 (D-Trp instead of Gly) and at the C-terminus (NHEt instead of Gly-NH₂). These modifications confer approximately 144-fold greater potency than native GnRH at the GnRH receptor (GnRHR) and dramatic resistance to peptidase-mediated degradation, yielding a sustained-action receptor agonist. Like other GnRH superagonists (leuprorelin, buserelin, goserelin), deslorelin causes an initial 'flare' of LH and FSH release followed by profound pituitary desensitization and receptor downregulation with continued non-pulsatile exposure, ultimately leading to hypogonadal testosterone/estradiol levels.\n\nDeslorelin is approved as a subcutaneous implant (Suprelorin®) in companion animals — particularly dogs, ferrets, and horses — for suppression of reproductive function. In horses it is used for induction of ovulation via the initial LH surge, while in dogs and ferrets it provides long-term reversible chemical castration for managing sex hormone-dependent conditions including benign prostatic hyperplasia, perineal hernias, and sex hormone-dependent alopecia. In veterinary endocrinology research, deslorelin implants serve as standardized tools for studying the HPG axis desensitization timeline, recovery of reproductive function after implant removal, and species-specific differences in GnRHR downregulation kinetics.\n\nIn translational research, deslorelin and related GnRH superagonists are studied for potential applications in prostate cancer (hormonal deprivation therapy), precocious puberty, endometriosis, uterine fibroids, and assisted reproductive technology (ART) protocols where pituitary suppression before controlled ovarian hyperstimulation is required. Deslorelin research has contributed to understanding the signaling cascades downstream of GnRHR occupancy that distinguish agonist-induced receptor internalization (beta-arrestin-mediated) from antagonist-blocked receptor states, informing the development of GnRH peptide pharmaceuticals with tailored receptor engagement profiles.",
