    shortDescription: "A synthetic analog of arginine vasopressin with enhanced antidiuretic activity, used in research on V2 receptor-mediated renal water reabsorption.",
    fullDescription: "Desmopressin (DDAVP, 1-desamino-8-D-arginine vasopressin) is a synthetic analog of arginine vasopressin (AVP) modified at two positions: the Cys1 amino group is removed (desamino modification, eliminating V1 pressor activity) and Arg8 is replaced by D-Arg (increasing V2 receptor selectivity and metabolic stability). These modifications result in a peptide with approximately 2,000-fold greater selectivity for V2 versus V1 receptors compared to native AVP, a 4-10 fold increase in antidiuretic potency, and a 2-3 fold prolonged duration of action (half-life ~75 min IV vs ~5 min for AVP). Desmopressin is thus a highly selective V2 receptor agonist, minimizing vasopressor effects while maximizing antidiuretic and hemostatic efficacy.\n\nDesmopressin activates V2 receptors (Gs-coupled) in renal collecting duct principal cells to stimulate cAMP/PKA-mediated AQP2 aquaporin phosphorylation, driving insertion of AQP2 water channels into the apical membrane and increasing collecting duct water permeability — the molecular basis of antidiuresis. In addition, V2 receptor activation in vascular endothelium triggers exocytosis of Weibel-Palade bodies containing von Willebrand factor (vWF) and Factor VIII, increasing circulating levels of both hemostatic factors by 3-5 fold within 30-60 minutes. This hemostatic mechanism makes desmopressin clinically useful in mild hemophilia A, Type 1 von Willebrand disease, and uremic platelet dysfunction.\n\nDesmopressin diacetate is used extensively in research on aquaporin-2 trafficking biology, V2 receptor signal transduction, hereditary nephrogenic diabetes insipidus (NDI) caused by AVPR2 or AQP2 mutations, primary nocturnal enuresis mechanisms, and SIADH/hyponatremia management. It is also used as a pharmacological tool in studies distinguishing V2-mediated renal effects from V1a-mediated cardiovascular effects of vasopressin. Desmopressin challenge tests using the diacetate salt are employed in research protocols to assess residual V2 receptor function in patients with partial NDI and to evaluate pharmacological rescue by V2 receptor chaperone compounds.",
