    fullDescription: "Eptifibatide (Integrilin) is a synthetic cyclic heptapeptide integrin αIIbβ3 antagonist derived from the disintegrin barbourin, a protein found in the venom of the southeastern pygmy rattlesnake (Sistrurus miliarius barbouri). Barbourin differs from other snake venom disintegrins in containing a Lys-Gly-Asp (KGD) motif rather than the typical Arg-Gly-Asp (RGD), providing selectivity for αIIbβ3 over other RGD-binding integrins (αVβ3, α5β1). Eptifibatide incorporates a non-natural homoarginine (Har) residue and a mercaptopropionic acid (Mpa) N-terminus in place of the cysteine, and a C-terminal amide forming a disulfide-bridged cyclic structure that mimics the KGD/RGD loop conformation of disintegrins.\n\nEptifibatide reversibly antagonizes platelet integrin αIIbβ3 (GPIIb/IIIa), which mediates the final common pathway of platelet aggregation by binding fibrinogen and von Willebrand factor crosslinks between platelets. By competitively blocking the RGD/KGD recognition site of αIIbβ3 — the Asp (D) in its KGD-containing loop binding the metal-ion-dependent adhesion site (MIDAS) of the β3 subunit — eptifibatide rapidly and reversibly inhibits platelet-platelet bridging, with recovery of platelet function within 4-6 hours of drug cessation due to its short plasma half-life (~2.5 h). FDA-approved as Integrilin, eptifibatide is used clinically for acute coronary syndrome and PCI.\n\nEptifibatide acetate is used in platelet biology research to study αIIbβ3 integrin signaling downstream of receptor blockade, including outside-in signaling (Src kinase activation, cytoskeletal reorganization), inside-out signaling pathways (talin and kindlin integrin activation), and the relationship between platelet aggregation inhibition and anti-thrombotic efficacy in flow chamber models. It serves as a pharmacological probe for dissecting αIIbβ3-dependent versus αIIbβ3-independent platelet functions, and as a reference compound for structure-activity relationship studies of RGD-mimetic peptidomimetic integrin antagonists. Research using eptifibatide has contributed to understanding the structural biology of integrin-ligand interactions and to the development of next-generation αIIbβ3 antagonists with improved selectivity and longer duration of action.",
