    fullDescription: "Glucagon is a 29-amino acid pancreatic peptide hormone secreted by alpha cells of the islets of Langerhans in response to hypoglycemia, prolonged fasting, and adrenergic stimulation. It is synthesized from proglucagon (a 160 aa precursor encoded by the GCG gene) through cleavage by prohormone convertase 2 (PC2), the same precursor that also generates GLP-1 and GLP-2 in intestinal L-cells through the action of PC1/3. Glucagon is the primary counter-regulatory hormone opposing insulin: through activation of the Gs-coupled glucagon receptor (GCGR) on hepatocytes, glucagon stimulates adenylate cyclase, elevating hepatic cAMP and activating PKA to promote glycogenolysis (glycogen phosphorylase activation), gluconeogenesis (PEPCK and glucose-6-phosphatase induction), and ketogenesis (beta-oxidation of fatty acids).\n\nGlucagon also modulates extra-hepatic tissues through GCGR and cross-reactivity with GLP-1 receptors at supraphysiological concentrations. In the heart, glucagon exerts positive inotropic and chronotropic effects through myocardial GCGR, making it a treatment for beta-blocker and calcium channel blocker overdose. In adipose tissue, GCGR activation promotes lipolysis, increasing circulating free fatty acids as substrates for hepatic ketogenesis. In the gastrointestinal tract, glucagon inhibits motility. Research using glucagon has elucidated the dual role of the GCG gene in glucose homeostasis — hepatic glucagon hyperglucagonemia being a central contributor to fasting hyperglycemia in type 2 diabetes — and has informed the development of GCGR antagonists and GLP-1R/GCGR dual agonists for metabolic disease.\n\nGlucagon is used in research applications including glucose counterregulation studies, hypoglycemia clamp experiments, hepatic glucose production assays, beta-cell function assessment (glucagon secretion during hyperinsulinemic clamps), cardiac pharmacology (GCGR-mediated inotropic effects), metabolic disease modeling, and evaluation of glucagon secretion kinetics in T1D/T2D. As a reference compound, synthetic glucagon is essential for validating glucagon immunoassays, characterizing GCGR binding kinetics, and studying the biochemistry of proglucagon processing and post-translational modifications.",
