    fullDescription: "Gonadorelin (GnRH, gonadotropin-releasing hormone, also called LHRH — luteinizing hormone-releasing hormone) is the native hypothalamic decapeptide that constitutes the primary signal controlling pituitary gonadotropin secretion. It is synthesized in GnRH neurons of the preoptic area and hypothalamic arcuate nucleus and released into the hypothalamic-hypophyseal portal circulation in a pulsatile fashion — approximately every 60–90 minutes in normally cycling individuals. This pulsatile pattern is essential: LH secretion requires GnRH pulses (high-frequency pulses favor LH over FSH), while sustained non-pulsatile GnRH receptor stimulation paradoxically causes pituitary desensitization and gonadotropin suppression, a phenomenon exploited clinically by GnRH superagonist analogs.\n\nGonadorelin acts on pituitary gonadotrophs through the GnRH receptor (GnRHR, Gq/11-coupled), activating phospholipase C/IP3/DAG signaling to stimulate calcium-dependent exocytosis of pre-formed LH granules (causing the acute LH surge) and induction of LHβ, FSHβ, and αGSU gene transcription (contributing to a delayed FSH peak). The LH surge triggers ovulation in females and testosterone synthesis stimulation in males. Gonadorelin diacetate is the salt form used for parenteral administration and for in vitro receptor studies.\n\nIn research and clinical practice, gonadorelin is used as a diagnostic tool to assess hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal function — the gonadorelin stimulation test (GnRH stimulation test) evaluates gonadotroph reserve and GnRH receptor responsiveness in hypogonadotropic hypogonadism, pituitary tumors, and delayed puberty. As a research tool, it is used in pituitary cell culture models to study GnRHR signal transduction, desensitization kinetics, beta-arrestin recruitment, and receptor internalization. In veterinary medicine, gonadorelin is used in Ovsynch protocols for synchronization of ovulation in cattle and other livestock. The diacetate salt provides improved aqueous solubility and stability for solution formulation and laboratory use.",
