    fullDescription: "Sermorelin (hGHRH(1-29)-NH₂) is a 29-amino acid C-terminally amidated fragment of human growth hormone-releasing hormone corresponding to the biologically active N-terminal region. Systematic truncation studies established that the minimal GHRH fragment retaining full GHRHR binding affinity and GH-stimulating potency is residues 1-29 — the C-terminal extension (residues 30-44) found in naturally secreted GHRH and in somatorelin (hGHRH(1-44)-NH₂) contributes stability but is not essential for biological activity. Sermorelin (approved as Geref in the US; now discontinued for growth hormone deficiency but available as a research compound) was the first GHRH analog approved for clinical use, serving as both a GH stimulation test agent and a treatment for GH-deficient growth failure.\n\nSermorelin activates the GHRHR through Gs/cAMP/PKA signaling in pituitary somatotrophs, stimulating GH gene transcription and the release of pre-formed GH secretory granules. Compared to longer forms of GHRH (1-40, 1-44), sermorelin's shorter length contributes to greater chemical stability during synthesis and storage, and the 1-29 truncation minimally impacts potency while providing a more practical research tool. Sermorelin-stimulated GH secretion is pulsatile and somatostatin-regulated, preserving the physiological feedback regulation absent from exogenous GH replacement, which is a theoretical advantage for maintaining GH axis responsiveness during treatment.\n\nSermorelin is used extensively in research on GHRH receptor pharmacology, pituitary somatotroph function, GH axis biology, and IGF-1-mediated growth regulation. The sermorelin GH stimulation test (GHRH-arginine test when combined with arginine infusion) is used clinically and in research to assess pituitary GH reserve and distinguish hypothalamic from pituitary-origin GH deficiency. Research applications include GH secretion dynamics modeling, GHRHR structure-activity studies, study of age-related GH decline (somatopause), and investigation of GHRH analog effects on body composition (fat mass vs. lean mass), bone mineral density, and cognitive function in GH-deficient states. Sermorelin research informed the development of longer-acting GHRH analogs including tesamorelin (approved for HIV-associated lipodystrophy).",
