    fullDescription: "Somatostatin (SS-14, growth hormone-inhibiting hormone, GHIH) is a 14-amino acid cyclic peptide containing a disulfide bridge between Cys3 and Cys14 that forms a 12-residue ring essential for biological activity. It was originally isolated from ovine hypothalamic extracts in 1972 by Guillemin's group during the search for GHRH and was found to inhibit GH secretion — hence its original name. Somatostatin is produced by hypothalamic neurons (D-cells), pancreatic delta cells, intestinal enteroendocrine cells, and parafollicular thyroid C-cells, and acts as a broad inhibitory hormone/neuromodulator through five GPCR subtypes (SSTR1-5, all Gi-coupled) distributed throughout the body.\n\nSomatostatin exerts pleiotropic inhibitory effects: in the pituitary, it inhibits GH and TSH secretion; in the pancreas, it suppresses insulin (SSTR5) and glucagon (SSTR2) secretion from beta and alpha cells, respectively; in the GI tract, it inhibits the secretion of virtually all gut hormones (gastrin, CCK, secretin, GIP, VIP, motilin) and reduces intestinal motility; in the nervous system, it functions as a neuromodulator inhibiting neuronal firing and neurotransmitter release in the cerebral cortex, hippocampus, and striatum; in peripheral tissues, it inhibits exocrine pancreatic secretion and reduces portal blood flow. Notably, native SS-14 has a very short plasma half-life of approximately 1-3 minutes due to rapid peptidase degradation, limiting its therapeutic utility.\n\nSomatostatin is used in research on GH/IGF-1 axis regulation (pituitary somatotroph suppression), pancreatic islet hormone secretion (insulin/glucagon balance), GI endocrinology (gut hormone suppression, motility regulation), neuromodulation studies (cortical and hippocampal SSTR signaling), and neuroendocrine tumor biology (carcinoids and gastrinomas express SSTR2/3/5 — basis for somatostatin analog imaging and therapy). The development of long-acting somatostatin analogs (octreotide, lanreotide, pasireotide) for acromegaly, carcinoid syndrome, and Cushing's disease was informed by SAR studies using SS-14 as the reference agonist. Research on SSTR subtype selectivity using SS-14 and synthetic analogs has characterized the signaling pathways for each receptor subtype and their distinct tissue-specific functions.",
