    fullDescription: "Argireline (Acetyl hexapeptide-3; Ac-EEMQRR-NH₂) is a synthetic hexapeptide that mimics the N-terminal sequence of SNAP-25 (synaptosomal-associated protein 25), a component of the SNARE complex essential for neurotransmitter vesicle fusion at the neuromuscular junction. By competing with endogenous SNAP-25 for incorporation into the SNARE ternary complex (syntaxin-1/SNAP-25/synaptobrevin), Argireline interferes with vesicle docking and fusion, thereby reducing acetylcholine release and attenuating muscle contraction. This competitive SNARE inhibition is proposed as the mechanistic basis for Argireline's cosmetic effect of reducing the appearance of expression-related facial lines, analogous in principle to botulinum toxin but without enzymatic cleavage of SNARE proteins.\n\nIn vitro neuromuscular studies using PC12 cells stimulated with potassium chloride demonstrate that Argireline at micromolar concentrations reduces catecholamine secretion by 30–40%, consistent with SNARE complex interference. Fluorescence resonance energy transfer (FRET) experiments have confirmed direct binding of Argireline to the SNAP-25 N-terminal SNARE motif, and competitive displacement assays show it reduces SNARE complex formation in a dose-dependent manner. Electrophysiology studies on primary motor neuron co-cultures with muscle cells have shown that Argireline treatment reduces end-plate potential amplitude without affecting resting membrane potential, supporting a pre-synaptic mechanism of action at the vesicular release step rather than post-synaptic receptor blockade.\n\nIn cosmetic research, Argireline is applied topically at 5–10% concentrations in ex vivo human skin models and 3D reconstructed epidermis systems to study its capacity to penetrate the stratum corneum and reach underlying dermal-epidermal junction structures. Radiolabeled tracer studies confirm limited but measurable penetration. Quantitative digital photogrammetry and wrinkle profilometry studies using 3D optical profiling have documented measurable improvements in periorbital rhytide depth after Argireline treatment in volunteer studies, providing instrumental evidence for SNARE-targeting peptides as topical neurocosmetic tools. Its well-defined sequence, small molecular weight, and established safety profile make Argireline a standard positive control in research evaluating novel neuromuscular blocking peptides.",
