    fullDescription: "Nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide (NAD⁺) is an essential dinucleotide coenzyme that shuttles electrons between metabolic reactions as a hydride acceptor/donor and serves as a substrate consumed by a diverse array of regulatory enzymes. As the oxidized form (NAD⁺), it accepts hydride equivalents during glycolysis, the TCA cycle, and beta-oxidation, becoming NADH, which subsequently donates electrons to Complex I of the mitochondrial electron transport chain to drive ATP synthesis via oxidative phosphorylation. The ratio of NAD⁺/NADH is a fundamental metabolic signal that reflects cellular energy status and regulates the activity of NAD-sensing enzymes including class III HDACs (sirtuins) and poly(ADP-ribose) polymerases (PARPs).\n\nSirtuins (SIRT1-7) are NAD⁺-dependent deacylases that consume NAD⁺ stoichiometrically during each catalytic cycle, producing nicotinamide (NAM) and 2'-O-acetyl-ADP-ribose as byproducts. Research using NAD⁺-depleted cell models and NAD⁺-biosynthesis inhibitors has demonstrated that sirtuin activity is directly limited by NAD⁺ availability in aging cells, where NAD⁺ levels decline by 40–60% compared with young tissue. SIRT1 deacetylation of PGC-1α activates mitochondrial biogenesis, and SIRT3 deacetylation of electron transport chain components modulates mitochondrial efficiency — linking NAD⁺ levels directly to mitochondrial bioenergetic function. These findings have positioned NAD⁺ precursors (NMN, NR) as research tools for restoring sirtuin activity in aged tissues.\n\nPARP enzymes consume NAD⁺ as the ADP-ribose donor for protein PARylation in response to DNA strand breaks, and hyperactivation of PARP1 during severe genotoxic stress can deplete NAD⁺ pools sufficiently to cause NAD⁺-depletion cell death (parthanatos). Research combining PARP inhibitors with NAD⁺ supplementation has clarified the NAD⁺ partition between DNA repair, sirtuin-mediated metabolic regulation, and mitochondrial energy generation in aging and cancer models. Studies in CD38-knockout mice — which exhibit 2–3-fold higher tissue NAD⁺ levels — have further demonstrated that CD38 NADase activity is a major determinant of NAD⁺ bioavailability in vivo, making CD38 inhibition an active area of research for NAD⁺ restoration strategies.",
