    fullDescription: "Murepavadin (POL7080/RG7929) is a synthetic macrocyclic lipopeptide that targets LptD, the outer membrane protein responsible for the final step in lipopolysaccharide (LPS) transport and assembly in gram-negative bacteria. LptD is essential for outer membrane integrity in Pseudomonas aeruginosa and other gram-negative pathogens; its disruption prevents LPS insertion into the outer leaflet, causing bacterial cell death through outer membrane dysfunction. Murepavadin was designed using a macrocyclic beta-hairpin scaffold incorporating non-natural amino acids and an N-terminal lipophilic tail, derived from the natural peptide protegrin-I framework but re-engineered for LptD selectivity rather than generic membrane disruption. In vitro assays confirm bactericidal activity against P. aeruginosa with MIC values of 0.06–0.25 μg/mL, with negligible activity against gram-positive bacteria and other gram-negative species — reflecting the high selectivity of LptD inhibition.\n\nResearch with murepavadin has illuminated the LPS transport machinery as a targetable vulnerability in P. aeruginosa, a high-priority resistant pathogen. Time-lapse electron microscopy studies show characteristic blebs and outer membrane vesicle shedding after murepavadin treatment, consistent with LPS packing defects. Biochemical experiments confirm that murepavadin inhibits the LptD–LptE plug-barrel interaction required for LPS extraction from the periplasmic LptA-C transport complex and flipping to the outer leaflet. These mechanistic studies have validated LptD as an antibacterial target and demonstrated that outer membrane biogenesis can be selectively inhibited without relying on membrane-disrupting mechanisms that cause off-target mammalian cell toxicity.\n\nMurepavadin has been evaluated in Phase II clinical trials for hospital-acquired pneumonia caused by P. aeruginosa, providing a clinical-stage antimicrobial tool for studying treatment of drug-resistant gram-negative pulmonary infections in research models. Its Pseudomonas selectivity also makes it a useful research tool for selectively depleting or identifying P. aeruginosa in polymicrobial infection models, and for studying the relationship between outer membrane integrity and antibiotic susceptibility in combination regimens. Research on murepavadin resistance mechanisms has identified mutations in the lptD and lptE genes that confer resistance, providing insight into the evolutionary pressure exerted by outer membrane biogenesis inhibitors.",
