    fullDescription: "Valspodar (PSC833) is a non-immunosuppressive cyclosporin D analog developed as a potent inhibitor of P-glycoprotein (P-gp/MDR1/ABCB1), the ATP-dependent drug efflux pump that is a primary mechanism of multidrug resistance (MDR) in cancer cells. Unlike its parent compound cyclosporin A, valspodar binds P-gp's drug-binding pocket with high affinity but does not inhibit calcineurin, the immunosuppressive target of cyclosporin A, making it a pharmacologically cleaner tool for studying P-gp inhibition without confounding immunosuppressive effects. Competitive transport assays using calcein-AM and rhodamine 123 efflux in MDR-overexpressing cell lines (e.g., KB-V1, MCF-7/ADR) confirm that valspodar reverses P-gp-mediated drug efflux at submicromolar concentrations, restoring intracellular accumulation of anthracyclines, taxanes, and vinca alkaloids to levels seen in P-gp-null cells.\n\nIn combinatorial chemotherapy resistance research, valspodar has been used to definitively attribute observed drug resistance to P-gp activity rather than other MDR mechanisms (e.g., MRP1/ABCC1, BCRP/ABCG2) by using selective transport assay conditions with fluorescent P-gp substrates. This pharmacological dissection capability has been essential for stratifying patient tumor biopsies by resistance mechanism in research biomarker studies. ATPase stimulation assays with purified recombinant P-gp confirm that valspodar acts as a transport inhibitor rather than a stimulator, blocking the substrate translocation cycle after initial binding. Photo-affinity crosslinking studies further demonstrate that valspodar binds within the large, promiscuous drug-binding cavity of P-gp's transmembrane domains — the same site occupied by chemotherapeutic substrates — confirming competitive inhibition as the dominant molecular mechanism.\n\nDespite promising preclinical results, Phase III clinical trials combining valspodar with chemotherapy in AML and advanced breast cancer showed no survival benefit and worsened toxicity from chemotherapy agent pharmacokinetic interactions — valspodar inhibits CYP3A4 and P-gp in the intestine and liver, reducing clearance of co-administered chemotherapy substrates and increasing systemic exposure to toxic levels. These outcomes established valspodar as a paradigm case study for the challenges of clinical P-gp inhibition and catalyzed research into third-generation P-gp inhibitors (tariquidar, elacridar) with better pharmacokinetic profiles. Valspodar remains a valuable research tool for in vitro mechanistic P-gp inhibition studies, transport kinetics characterization, and as a reference compound for benchmarking novel MDR reversal agents.",
