    fullDescription: "Taspoglutide (R1583/RO5073031) is a long-acting GLP-1 receptor agonist developed by Roche that incorporates two alpha-aminoisobutyric acid (Aib) substitutions at positions 2 and 19 of the native GLP-1 sequence. These Aib residues impose conformational rigidity on the peptide backbone, conferring resistance to DPP-4 cleavage at the His¹-Aib² bond and providing resistance to endopeptidase degradation throughout the peptide chain. A C18 fatty acid was further conjugated to enable albumin binding and extend plasma half-life to approximately 7 days, enabling once-weekly dosing for research purposes. Mechanistic studies in GLP-1R–expressing CHO cells confirm that taspoglutide activates GLP-1R with subnanomolar potency (EC₅₀ ~0.02 nM for cAMP generation), comparable to native GLP-1(7-37) but with dramatically improved metabolic stability.\n\nIn Phase II clinical studies, taspoglutide demonstrated statistically significant reductions in HbA1c (1.0–1.2% reduction) and body weight (2–3 kg loss) compared to placebo in type 2 diabetes patients when administered once weekly, establishing proof-of-concept for Aib-based GLP-1R agonists as a viable drug class. In rodent and primate metabolic research models, taspoglutide reproduces the full profile of GLP-1R agonism: glucose-stimulated insulin secretion, glucagon suppression, gastric emptying delay, and hypothalamic appetite suppression. Importantly, taspoglutide's Aib substitutions make it useful in research for studying GLP-1R conformational biology — the Aib residues stabilize the peptide in a helical conformation that may favor specific receptor interaction geometries, providing structural insights into GLP-1R activation distinct from those obtained with DPP-4-susceptible native GLP-1.\n\nTaspoglutide's development was ultimately discontinued in Phase III due to a high incidence of gastrointestinal adverse events (nausea, vomiting) and immune-mediated hypersensitivity reactions that were hypothesized to result from the peptide's high immunogenicity relative to more humanized GLP-1 analogs. This experience has informed the design of subsequent GLP-1R agonists to minimize immunogenic epitopes while maintaining DPP-4 resistance. In research settings, taspoglutide serves as a valuable comparator compound for studying differences between structural classes of GLP-1R agonists (Aib-modified vs. fatty acid-conjugated native sequences vs. small-molecule agonists) in terms of receptor engagement kinetics, beta-arrestin recruitment, and in vivo pharmacodynamic duration-response relationships.",
