    fullDescription: "Ularitide (urodilatin; ANP[95-126]) is a 32-amino acid natriuretic peptide produced in renal tubular cells, first isolated from human urine, and structurally identical to atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP) except for an N-terminal 4-amino acid extension (Thr-Ala-Pro-Arg) not present in circulating ANP. This extension renders ularitide resistant to neutral endopeptidase (NEP/neprilysin) cleavage at the kidney tubular surface, allowing it to exert potent natriuretic and diuretic effects locally in the renal tubule while circulating ANP is rapidly degraded. Like all natriuretic peptides, ularitide acts on NPR-A, a membrane-bound guanylyl cyclase receptor, stimulating intracellular cGMP production that activates protein kinase G (PKG) to phosphorylate target proteins including aquaporin-2, the epithelial sodium channel (ENaC), and myosin light chain phosphatase in vascular smooth muscle.\n\nIn cardiovascular research, ularitide has been studied as a treatment candidate for acute decompensated heart failure (ADHF), where reduced renal perfusion and elevated circulating neurohumoral factors (aldosterone, angiotensin II, endothelin-1) cause fluid retention and cardiac pressure overload. Ularitide infusion in rodent and large-animal heart failure models produces dose-dependent reductions in pulmonary capillary wedge pressure, right atrial pressure, and systemic vascular resistance, while increasing urine volume and sodium excretion — providing a research tool for studying natriuretic peptide hemodynamics and renal unloading effects. These pharmacodynamic readouts have been used to study the relationship between cGMP signaling in the kidney and the cardiovascular neurohumoral response in heart failure.\n\nUlaritide was evaluated in the Phase III TRUE-AHF trial for acute heart failure and, despite showing expected hemodynamic and natriuretic effects, did not improve clinical outcomes (mortality or rehospitalization) relative to placebo. This outcome prompted research into why acute natriuretic peptide therapy fails to translate hemodynamic benefits into clinical outcomes, and has driven studies on timing, dosing, patient selection, and whether concurrent neurohormonal activation limits the net clinical benefit of acute natriuresis. In basic research settings, ularitide's known renal origin and NEP resistance make it a useful tool for studying the intrarenal natriuretic peptide system separately from circulating ANP, with applications in understanding paracrine cGMP signaling in the tubuloglomerular feedback axis and the cross-talk between renal and cardiac natriuretic peptide physiology.",
