    fullDescription: "Rusfertide (PTG-300) is a synthetic hepcidin mimetic peptide developed by Protagonist Therapeutics to replicate the activity of the endogenous iron-regulatory hormone hepcidin. Endogenous hepcidin (a 25-amino acid defensin-like peptide) controls systemic iron homeostasis by binding to and inducing the internalization and lysosomal degradation of ferroportin (FPN1/SLC40A1), the sole known iron exporter on enterocytes, hepatocytes, and macrophages. By mimicking hepcidin's ferroportin-binding activity, rusfertide reduces intestinal iron absorption and prevents macrophage iron recycling, leading to decreased plasma transferrin saturation and suppressed erythropoiesis. Subcutaneous rusfertide administration in rodent iron overload models consistently normalizes serum iron and hepcidin biomarker profiles within 24–48 hours, validating ferroportin inhibition as the primary mechanism.\n\nIn polycythemia vera (PV) research — a myeloproliferative neoplasm characterized by excess erythrocytosis, elevated hematocrit, and thrombotic risk — rusfertide has been studied as an iron-restriction strategy to control erythropoiesis without requiring phlebotomy. JAK2-V617F knock-in mouse models of PV treated with rusfertide show reduced red blood cell mass and hematocrit normalization, accompanied by decreased plasma transferrin saturation and iron-restricted erythropoiesis as indicated by increased hypochromic red cell percentages. Phase I/II REVIVE clinical trial data demonstrated that rusfertide maintained target hematocrit (<45%) with reduced phlebotomy frequency and improved patient-reported fatigue scores compared to phlebotomy alone, providing clinical translation of hepcidin mimicry research findings.\n\nRusfertide is also used in research on transfusion-dependent beta-thalassemia and other iron overload disorders where endogenous hepcidin production is abnormally suppressed (by erythroferrone and GDF15 released from ineffective erythropoiesis). By restoring ferroportin inhibition despite depressed endogenous hepcidin, rusfertide research models have clarified the dominant role of erythropoiesis-driven iron absorption dysregulation in secondary iron overload, and have provided a tool for studying whether exogenous hepcidin agonism reduces iron burden and iron-mediated organ damage in chronic anemia models independently of phlebotomy or chelation.",
