    fullDescription: "Nerinetide (NA-1; Tat-NR2B9c) is a neuroprotective peptide designed to disrupt the ternary excitotoxic signaling complex formed by activated NMDA receptors (NMDARs), PSD-95 (postsynaptic density protein 95), and neuronal nitric oxide synthase (nNOS). Upon excessive NMDAR activation during ischemic or excitotoxic conditions, glutamate-induced Ca²⁺ influx leads to nNOS activation through a scaffold maintained by PSD-95, producing toxic quantities of nitric oxide (NO) that drive peroxynitrite formation and oxidative neuronal damage. Nerinetide's NR2B9c domain (derived from the C-terminal PDZ-binding sequence of the NMDA receptor NR2B subunit) competitively displaces NR2B from its PSD-95 PDZ2 domain interaction, breaking the NMDAR-PSD-95-nNOS signaling complex without blocking NMDAR ion channel function — thereby uncoupling excitotoxic downstream signaling while preserving physiological NMDAR neurotransmission.\n\nIn rodent ischemia models, nerinetide administered at reperfusion significantly reduces infarct volume, neurological deficit scores, and neuronal death in the ischemic penumbra. Time-course studies confirm that PSD-95 disruption provides a 1–3 hour therapeutic window after onset of ischemia in animal models, correlating with the period of penumbral tissue salvageability. Unlike direct NMDAR blockers (e.g., MK-801, memantine), nerinetide does not impair cognitive function in behavioral tests at effective neuroprotective doses, as it preserves NMDAR-mediated synaptic plasticity while blocking downstream excitotoxic signaling. Fluorescence imaging with Cy3-labeled nerinetide in hippocampal slice preparations confirms rapid synaptic targeting to PSD-enriched postsynaptic compartments.\n\nIn the Phase IIb/III ESCAPE-NA1 clinical trial for ischemic stroke, nerinetide administered at endovascular thrombectomy did not achieve the primary outcome in the overall population but showed statistically significant benefit in patients not receiving tPA — a finding interpreted as reflecting nerinetide-tPA pharmacological interaction (tPA-enhanced plasmin may proteolytically cleave peptide bonds in nerinetide). This interaction has driven subsequent research into nerinetide analog design with protease-resistant modifications and studies on nerinetide's mechanism in the context of clinical thrombolysis. The peptide remains a key research tool for studying PSD-95 scaffold biology, synaptic signaling complex pharmacology, and the cell-penetrating TAT peptide delivery system for CNS drug targeting.",
