    fullDescription: "Cagrilintide (AM833) is a novel, long-acting amylin receptor agonist developed by Novo Nordisk for research into combined amylin + GLP-1R agonism for obesity and type 2 diabetes. Amylin (islet amyloid polypeptide/IAPP) is a 37-amino acid peptide co-secreted with insulin from pancreatic beta-cells that slows gastric emptying, suppresses postprandial glucagon secretion, and promotes satiety through amylin receptor complexes (calcitonin receptor + RAMP proteins) in the area postrema/nucleus tractus solitarius (AP/NTS) and hypothalamus. Cagrilintide incorporates a C18 fatty diacid conjugate for albumin binding, Aib substitutions for metabolic stability, and a salmon calcitonin-derived scaffold that retains full amylin receptor agonism while eliminating the amyloidogenic properties of native IAPP (which forms pancreatic amyloid in type 2 diabetes). Once-weekly SC dosing achieves sustained amylin receptor activation across the dosing interval in primate models.\n\nCagrilintide has been studied both as a monotherapy and in combination with semaglutide (CagriSema combination) in Phase II/III research. As monotherapy in DIO models, cagrilintide produces dose-dependent weight reduction of 6-10% over 12-16 weeks in rodent models, primarily through hypothalamic satiety signaling that reduces meal frequency and meal size. The combination of cagrilintide + semaglutide (CagriSema) in non-human primate models and Phase II clinical trials produces synergistic weight loss exceeding that of either agent alone — the SCALE NEXT Phase III program demonstrated approximately 22-24% mean body weight reduction with CagriSema versus approximately 15% with semaglutide alone, consistent with non-overlapping satiety mechanisms (amylin receptor AP/NTS pathway versus GLP-1R vagal/hypothalamic pathway). This synergy has been mechanistically studied using central neural circuit mapping with c-Fos immunostaining, confirming distinct but converging neuronal activation patterns for amylin and GLP-1 satiety signaling.\n\nCagrilintide is used in metabolic research to dissect the specific contributions of amylin receptor signaling to weight regulation, as distinct from GLP-1R agonism. Unlike GLP-1R agonists, amylin analogs do not directly stimulate insulin secretion or inhibit glucagon in a glucose-dependent manner but instead act centrally and in the GI tract to reduce caloric intake through complementary mechanisms. Research using cagrilintide in beta-cell-depleted (STZ) mouse models — where GLP-1R agonist insulinotropic effects are absent — confirms that amylin receptor agonism retains substantial weight-reducing efficacy independently of pancreatic insulin secretion, demonstrating the value of studying amylin receptor pharmacology as a distinct metabolic drug target.",
