    shortDescription: "A JNK inhibitor peptide (D-JNKI-1) used in research on inflammatory signaling, auditory hair cell protection, and neurodegeneration prevention.",
    fullDescription: "Brimapitide (D-JNKI-1; AM-111) is a D-amino acid retro-inverso peptide comprising the JNK inhibitory sequence derived from the JIP-1 (JNK-interacting protein 1) scaffold fused to the TAT cell-penetrating peptide sequence. The JIP-1-derived inhibitory sequence (JNKI-1) binds the substrate-docking groove (D-site) of JNK isoforms (JNK1/2/3) with high affinity (IC₅₀ 0.5–5 nM), competitively preventing substrate phosphorylation by JNK without affecting other kinases including ERK, p38, or PKC. D-amino acid composition confers protease resistance and extended intracellular half-life compared to L-amino acid JNKI-1 analogs, while the TAT sequence ensures cell membrane penetration and cytoplasmic delivery of the inhibitory domain without requiring specialized transfection systems. JNK is a central mediator of cellular stress responses including apoptosis, inflammatory cytokine production, and oxidative stress — making its selective inhibition a valuable research tool for dissecting JNK-dependent from JNK-independent stress signaling branches.\n\nBrimapitide has been extensively studied in cochlear hair cell protection research, where JNK activation is a critical early mediator of ototoxic (aminoglycoside, cisplatin) and acoustic trauma-induced hair cell apoptosis. In guinea pig cochlear explant models, brimapitide applied before or immediately after acoustic trauma preserves inner and outer hair cell counts, maintains compound action potential (CAP) and distortion product otoacoustic emission (DPOAE) thresholds, and prevents c-Jun nuclear translocation in spiral ganglion neurons — confirming JNK pathway inhibition as the protective mechanism. Based on these preclinical results, brimapitide (AM-111) entered clinical trials for acute acoustic trauma (sudden sensorineural hearing loss), with the HEALOS Phase II/III trial demonstrating statistically significant hearing threshold improvement compared to placebo in patients with moderate-to-severe acoustic trauma, representing the first successful clinical demonstration of an inner ear cytoprotective peptide for noise-induced hearing loss.\n\nBeyond auditory research, brimapitide is used to study JNK signaling in diverse contexts including cardiac ischemia-reperfusion injury, retinal neurodegeneration, cerebral ischemia, and inflammatory arthritis. In isolated cardiomyocyte models, brimapitide pre-treatment before simulated ischemia reduces cytochrome c release, caspase-3/7 activation, and mitochondrial membrane potential collapse downstream of JNK activation, providing mechanistic support for JNK as a mediator of ischemic cardiomyocyte death. Its well-defined D-amino acid structure, established cell-penetrating delivery, and validated JNK pharmacology make brimapitide one of the most extensively characterized intracellular peptide-based kinase inhibitors available for stress signaling pathway research.",
