    shortDescription: "A MUC1 mucin-derived peptide used in cancer research for studying immune evasion mechanisms and cancer vaccine development strategies.",
    fullDescription: "Asudemotide (BLP25 liposome vaccine; tecemotide) is an investigational cancer therapeutic vaccine centered on a lipopeptide consisting of a 25-amino acid MUC1 tandem repeat core peptide (BLP25) formulated in a liposome with DPPC, cholesterol, and monophosphoryl lipid A (MPL) as an adjuvant. MUC1 is a type 1 transmembrane mucin glycoprotein that is highly overexpressed and aberrantly glycosylated (hypoglycosylated, exposing the peptide core) on most epithelial cancers including non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC), breast cancer, colorectal cancer, and multiple myeloma, making the exposed tandem repeat core a tumor-associated antigen (TAA) not normally accessible in healthy tissue. The asudemotide vaccine aims to break immune tolerance to MUC1 by presenting the peptide core in an immunostimulatory lipid formulation, inducing both CD8+ CTL responses against MUC1-positive tumor cells and MUC1-specific antibody production.\n\nIn preclinical research using MUC1-transgenic mice — which express human MUC1 and are therefore partially tolerant to it — BLP25 liposome vaccine immunization generates detectable MUC1-specific IFN-γ-producing T-cells by ELISPOT assay and induces anti-MUC1 IgG antibodies with cross-reactivity to tumor cell lines. In experimental tumor models using MUC1-expressing CT26 colorectal carcinoma and 4T1 breast cancer syngeneic cells, asudemotide treatment delays tumor growth and reduces lung metastasis formation, correlating with increased intratumoral CD8+ T-cell infiltration. These preclinical results established MUC1 as a viable TAA for peptide-liposome vaccine strategies and informed clinical trial design for asudemotide in lung cancer patients.\n\nIn the Phase III START trial for unresectable stage III NSCLC, asudemotide administered after concurrent chemoradiotherapy did not achieve statistical significance for the primary overall survival endpoint in the overall trial population; however, retrospective analysis identified that cyclophosphamide pre-conditioning significantly altered immunological responses to vaccination, and a per-protocol subgroup not receiving cyclophosphamide showed improved survival trends. These findings have been used to study how concurrent immunosuppressive chemotherapy affects the outcome of therapeutic cancer vaccines, and have reinforced the importance of trial design elements including patient selection, concurrent therapy management, and immune monitoring endpoints for cancer vaccine research.",
