    fullDescription: "DiaPep277 is a 24-amino acid synthetic peptide corresponding to residues 437-460 of the human 60-kDa heat shock protein (Hsp60/HSPD1), known as the p277 peptide. Heat shock protein 60 plays dual roles in cellular homeostasis as an intracellular chaperone and as an extracellular danger signal that engages innate immune receptors including TLR2 and TLR4. In type 1 diabetes (T1D), Hsp60 expressed on stressed and apoptotic pancreatic beta-cells is recognized by autoreactive T-cells, and the Hsp60-specific immune response shifts from a Th2-regulatory pattern (present in healthy individuals) to a Th1-inflammatory pattern (associated with beta-cell destruction) in genetically predisposed individuals. The p277 (DiaPep277) peptide, administered in oil-based adjuvant or without adjuvant, is hypothesized to reverse this Hsp60-specific Th1/Th2 immune imbalance by stimulating TLR2 signaling on dendritic cells and promoting the induction of IL-4-secreting regulatory T-cells that suppress autoimmune beta-cell targeting.\n\nIn NOD (non-obese diabetic) mouse models, the prototypical animal model for T1D, subcutaneous immunization with p277 peptide significantly delays diabetes onset and reduces islet lymphocytic infiltration (insulitis) severity. Mechanistic studies in p277-treated NOD mice show increased Hsp60-specific IL-4 secretion and decreased IFN-γ production by splenocytes upon in vitro antigen restimulation, along with higher percentages of FoxP3+ regulatory T-cells in peripheral blood and pancreatic draining lymph nodes. These immunological shifts correlate with sustained C-peptide secretion (a marker of residual beta-cell mass) in treated versus control mice, confirming that altered Hsp60-specific immune regulation translates to functional beta-cell preservation.\n\nDiaPep277 was evaluated in Phase II and III clinical trials (DEFEND-1 and DEFEND-2) in newly diagnosed T1D patients, targeting a window of residual beta-cell mass when immune modulation might arrest ongoing autoimmune destruction. While the trials demonstrated encouraging signals of C-peptide preservation in subgroup analyses, particularly in male patients, the primary endpoint was not consistently achieved across both trials. Research with DiaPep277 has contributed significantly to understanding antigen-specific immunotherapy for T1D, the role of Hsp60 as a dual intracellular/extracellular immune modulator, TLR2 signaling in tolerance induction, and the methodological challenges of measuring immune tolerance induction in clinical trials through validated biomarkers.",
