    shortDescription: "A fluorescently labeled antimicrobial peptide analog used in imaging research for studying membrane interactions and bacterial targeting visualization.",
    fullDescription: "ALM-488 is a fluorescently labeled derivative of alamethicin, an Aib (alpha-aminoisobutyric acid)-rich 19-residue peptaibol produced by Trichoderma viride that forms voltage-dependent ion channels in lipid bilayers. Alamethicin's channel-forming activity has made it a paradigmatic model compound for studying the biophysics of peptide-induced membrane pores. Its high Aib content forces an alpha-helical secondary structure even in aqueous solution, and upon insertion into lipid bilayers at positive membrane potentials, multiple alamethicin monomers oligomerize to form barrel-stave pores with conductance states in the picosiemen-to-nanosiemen range detectable by single-channel electrophysiology. ALM-488 incorporates an AlexaFluor 488 fluorescent dye (excitation 495 nm, emission 519 nm) conjugated at the C-terminal glutamine residue, allowing direct visualization of the peptide's membrane association and insertion dynamics without requiring indirect labeling strategies.\n\nIn fluorescence microscopy research, ALM-488 enables real-time imaging of alamethicin membrane binding kinetics in giant unilamellar vesicles (GUVs) and supported lipid bilayers using total internal reflection fluorescence (TIRF) microscopy and confocal live imaging. Single-molecule fluorescence studies with ALM-488 have tracked individual peptide diffusion in the bilayer plane and oligomerization steps preceding pore formation, providing temporal resolution for studying the stochastic nature of alamethicin channel assembly. Fluorescence correlation spectroscopy (FCS) using ALM-488 in model membranes of defined composition has enabled measurement of membrane partitioning coefficients and lateral diffusion coefficients as a function of lipid phase (liquid-ordered vs. liquid-disordered domains), contributing to understanding how membrane physical chemistry influences alamethicin channel formation efficiency.\n\nALM-488 is used in antibacterial and antifungal research as a probe for studying membrane-active compound interactions with bacterial membranes in live cells. Confocal imaging of bacteria (E. coli, B. subtilis, S. aureus) co-incubated with ALM-488 at sub-MIC concentrations reveals membrane binding localization patterns, with preferential association to septal (division plane) membranes in dividing bacteria and localization to cardiolipin-enriched polar domains in some species — suggesting that membrane lipid composition and curvature influence alamethicin targeting. Comparative imaging with other fluorescently labeled antimicrobial peptides (melittin-488, magainin-488) using two-color TIRF enables direct visualization of competitive membrane binding and spatial segregation of different amphipathic peptides in the same bilayer.",
