    fullDescription: "Zilucoplan (RA101495) is a macrocyclic peptide inhibitor of complement component C5 developed by UCB and approved (as Zilbrysq) for generalized myasthenia gravis (gMG) treatment. Complement C5 is the central convergence point of complement activation, where proteolytic cleavage by C5 convertase generates C5a (a potent pro-inflammatory anaphylatoxin and chemoattractant for neutrophils and mast cells) and C5b (which initiates the terminal complement cascade leading to membrane attack complex/MAC formation and cell lysis). Zilucoplan binds C5 with picomolar affinity (Kd ~0.1 nM), sterically blocking the cleavage site recognized by both alternative and classical pathway C5 convertases, without affecting C1-C4 upstream complement components. Its macrocyclic peptide structure confers high selectivity for C5 over other complement proteins and pharmacokinetic properties appropriate for once-daily subcutaneous self-administration in clinical settings.\n\nIn myasthenia gravis research, the complement system plays a direct pathogenic role when IgG1/IgG3 class anti-AChR (acetylcholine receptor) antibodies deposit at the neuromuscular junction (NMJ) and activate complement, generating C5b-9 MAC that damages junctional folds and reduces AChR density, impairing neuromuscular transmission. Zilucoplan in in vitro NMJ models prevents MAC deposition and AChR degradation in antibody-treated muscle fiber preparations, and in passive transfer EAN (experimental autoimmune neuritis) models prevents NMJ dysfunction as measured by repetitive nerve stimulation electrophysiology. The Phase III RAISE trial in gMG demonstrated that once-daily SC zilucoplan significantly improved myasthenic daily living scores, grip strength, and quantitative MG scores versus placebo, validating complement C5 as a therapeutic target at the NMJ.\n\nBeyond gMG, zilucoplan is used in complement research broadly — including paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria (PNH), atypical hemolytic uremic syndrome (aHUS), lupus nephritis, and organ transplantation — as a subcutaneously deliverable alternative to IV anti-C5 monoclonal antibodies (eculizumab, ravulizumab). Its small molecular weight compared to antibody biologics enables SC self-injection and potentially more consistent subcutaneous bioavailability, providing a research model for studying the pharmacodynamic implications of complement inhibition delivery route and dosing frequency. Zilucoplan's well-characterized binding mechanism also makes it a valuable tool for studying C5 structural biology, C5 convertase kinetics, and the contribution of alternative versus classical pathway C5 activation to complement-mediated pathology in different disease models.",
