    fullDescription: "Motixafortide (BKT140) is a high-affinity, long-acting macrocyclic peptide antagonist of CXCR4 developed by BioLineRx, distinguished from other CXCR4 antagonists by its unique bicyclic disulfide-stabilized beta-hairpin structure that confers subnanomolar binding affinity (IC₅₀ ~0.3 nM) and extended receptor occupancy exceeding 24 hours in vivo. The CXCR4-CXCL12 (SDF-1α) axis is the dominant chemokine pathway retaining hematopoietic stem and progenitor cells (HSPCs) within bone marrow niches through CXCL12 gradients emanating from CXCL12-abundant reticular (CAR) cells and endosteal osteoblast-rich niches. By blocking CXCL12 binding with high affinity and prolonged duration, motixafortide generates a more sustained CXCR4 blockade than first-generation CXCR4 antagonists (plerixafor/AMD3100, which requires >4-hourly dosing for complete receptor block), enabling superior and more durable HSPC mobilization with fewer doses.\n\nIn clinical research for autologous stem cell transplantation in multiple myeloma, the Phase III GENESIS trial demonstrated that motixafortide + G-CSF achieved significantly higher CD34+ cell yields on the first apheresis day compared to placebo + G-CSF, with 93% versus 26% of patients reaching the threshold of ≥6×10⁶ CD34+ cells/kg in two apheresis sessions. This represented a major improvement in stem cell mobilization efficiency and has driven research into the cellular mechanisms by which sustained CXCR4 antagonism mobilizes HSPC subsets beyond those released by short-duration plerixafor. Flow cytometric HSPC phenotyping in motixafortide-mobilized products confirms enrichment of long-term repopulating (Lin-CD34+CD38-CD45RA-CD90+) HSC subsets compared to G-CSF alone, suggesting superior graft quality for engraftment research.\n\nIn oncology research, motixafortide is studied for its effects on disrupting CXCR4-dependent tumor cell retention in protective bone marrow niches — a mechanism through which multiple myeloma and AML cells evade apoptosis from cytotoxic therapy (chemotherapy protection/resistance via CXCL12 survival signaling). CXCR4 blockade by motixafortide in MM cell line-bone marrow stromal cell co-cultures reduces CXCL12-mediated phospho-AKT and phospho-ERK survival signaling and increases bortezomib/lenalidomide sensitivity, providing mechanistic support for combining CXCR4 antagonism with standard-of-care treatment. Motixafortide's extended receptor occupancy also makes it a useful tool for studying the timecourse of HSPC niche signaling recovery after CXCR4 blockade, including the kinetics of CXCL12 gradient re-establishment and HSC re-engraftment into marrow niches after apheresis.",
