    shortDescription: "A long-acting echinocandin antifungal peptide used in research on Candida and Aspergillus cell wall beta-glucan synthase inhibition.",
    fullDescription: "Rezafungin acetate (CD101) is a novel semi-synthetic echinocandin antifungal compound developed by Cidara Therapeutics, designed to achieve once-weekly intravenous dosing by optimization of the cyclic hexapeptide scaffold relative to existing echinocandins (caspofungin, micafungin, anidulafungin). Echinocandins are cyclic lipopeptides that non-competitively inhibit (1,3)-β-D-glucan synthase, the enzyme complex (composed of catalytic FKS1/FKS2 subunits and regulatory RHO1 subunit) responsible for polymerizing UDP-glucose into the structural (1,3)-β-D-glucan polymer that constitutes 50-60% of the fungal cell wall. Glucan depletion disrupts cell wall integrity, causes osmotic instability, and induces paradoxical chitin upregulation before eventual fungal cell lysis. Rezafungin's modified lipophilic tail and C-terminal amide contribute to its approximately 130-hour plasma half-life in humans, enabling once-weekly dosing that maintains plasma concentrations above the MIC throughout the dosing interval — a significant pharmacokinetic advantage for both outpatient use and concentration-dependent killing of Candida species.\n\nIn vitro susceptibility testing confirms rezafungin's fungicidal activity against Candida albicans, C. glabrata, C. parapsilosis (reduced susceptibility due to natural FKS polymorphisms), C. tropicalis, and C. auris — including many azole- and echinocandin-resistant strains. Against Aspergillus species, rezafungin demonstrates fungistatic activity consistent with the echinocandin class. Time-kill kinetics studies show rapid concentration-dependent killing of Candida species at concentrations of 2-4× the MIC, with a post-antifungal effect (PAE) exceeding 12 hours for Candida species tested, supporting the once-weekly dosing rationale. In murine models of disseminated candidiasis and Candida auris infection — the latter a CDC urgent threat pathogen — rezafungin significantly reduces fungal kidney and brain burden compared to vehicle controls, with efficacy maintained against FKS1-mutant echinocandin-resistant strains at elevated rezafungin concentrations.\n\nRezafungin (Rezzayo) received FDA approval for candidemia and invasive candidiasis treatment in adults unable to tolerate oral antifungal therapy. In research settings, rezafungin is used to study echinocandin pharmacodynamics (concentration-AUC/MIC relationships), FKS1/FKS2 mutation-mediated resistance mechanisms, and the implications of prolonged antifungal exposure intervals on fungal adaptation. Comparative studies with other echinocandins have mapped structure-activity relationships for the echinocandin lipopeptide scaffold, particularly how C-tail composition affects protein binding, half-life, and activity against FKS hot-spot mutations. Its once-weekly profile also makes rezafungin a model compound for studying antifungal pharmacokinetic-pharmacodynamic relationships in the context of prolonged low-frequency dosing versus high-frequency conventional dosing.",
