    fullDescription: "Trofinetide (NNZ-2566) is a synthetic analog of Gly-Pro-Glu (GPE), the N-terminal tripeptide of insulin-like growth factor-1 (IGF-1) that is cleaved from the full-length protein in the brain by a specific endoprotease. This tripeptide fragment retains significant neuroprotective properties despite having no IGF-1 receptor binding activity — its neuroprotection mechanisms are thought to involve direct modulation of glutamate receptor activity, reduction of neuroinflammatory cytokine release (particularly microglial IL-1β, TNF-α, and IL-6), and protection of synaptic density. Trofinetide is distinguished from native GPE by methylation of the N-terminal glycine residue (Gly-Pro-Glu → NMe-Gly-Pro-Glu), which substantially improves metabolic stability by preventing aminopeptidase N degradation, extending its half-life from minutes (for GPE) to hours and enabling meaningful oral bioavailability for research applications.\n\nIn Rett syndrome research, trofinetide addresses a key feature of this MECP2-mutation neurological disorder: progressive synaptic deficits and dendritic morphology abnormalities associated with microglial-driven neuroinflammation. In MECP2-deficient mouse models (the Mecp2-KO and Mecp2-heterozygous models), trofinetide treatment significantly reduces activated microglial markers (Iba-1, CD68), decreases brain IL-1β and TNF-α levels, and partially restores synapse density (synapsin-1 and PSD-95 puncta counts) in cortical and hippocampal regions. Behavioral assessments in Mecp2-heterozygous female mice show improvement in motor coordination (rotarod), hindlimb clasping frequency, and spontaneous breathing irregularity — core features of Rett syndrome — after chronic trofinetide treatment, providing the preclinical basis for clinical development.\n\nTrofinetide (Daybue) received FDA approval in 2023 for Rett syndrome — the first and only approved pharmacological treatment for this condition. This approval was based on the Phase III LAVENDER trial demonstrating significant improvements in the Rett Syndrome Behaviour Questionnaire (RSBQ) and Clinical Global Impressions-Improvement (CGI-I) scores versus placebo. In research settings, trofinetide serves as a tool for studying the pathophysiology of MECP2-related synaptic dysfunction, the role of neuroinflammation in Rett syndrome, and the mechanisms by which GPE-class tripeptides modulate glutamate receptor density and inflammatory cascades in neural tissue. Its utility extends to TBI research, where the neuroinflammatory component of secondary injury is similarly addressed in controlled cortical impact models.",
