    fullDescription: "Ziconotide (Prialt; ω-conotoxin MVIIA) is a 25-amino acid conopeptide originally isolated from the predatory cone snail Conus magus, containing three intramolecular disulfide bonds that create a highly constrained beta-hairpin structure resistant to proteolytic degradation. It is one of the most selective natural peptide inhibitors of N-type (Cav2.2) voltage-gated calcium channels, binding the outer vestibule of the channel with extremely high affinity (Kd ~0.01 nM) and blocking calcium ion influx with essentially no activity at L-type, P/Q-type, or R-type calcium channels. N-type calcium channels are critically expressed at the presynaptic terminals of primary afferent C-fibers and A-δ fibers at the spinal cord dorsal horn, where they mediate calcium-triggered release of substance P, CGRP, and glutamate onto dorsal horn neurons. By blocking Cav2.2, ziconotide prevents neurotransmitter release and interrupts nociceptive signal transmission at the spinal level, producing potent antinociception without tolerance development or opioid-type side effects.\n\nIn rodent pain research models, intrathecal ziconotide produces dose-dependent antinociception in the hot plate test, formalin test, carrageenan-induced inflammatory hyperalgesia model, and neuropathic pain models (spinal nerve ligation, chronic constriction injury). Unlike opioid analgesics, tolerance to ziconotide's antinociceptive effects does not develop with repeated intrathecal dosing in chronic pain models, consistent with the absence of receptor internalization or receptor downregulation for its voltage-gated ion channel target. Single-channel and whole-cell patch clamp electrophysiology in spinal cord dorsal horn neurons confirm that ziconotide selectively abolishes Cav2.2-mediated calcium currents at concentrations that do not affect Cav2.1 (P/Q-type) or Cav1 (L-type) channels, validating target engagement specificity.\n\nZiconotide (Prialt) was approved by the FDA in 2004 for intrathecal delivery to treat severe chronic pain in patients refractory to other therapies — the first and only approved intrathecal peptide analgesic. Its narrow therapeutic window via intrathecal delivery (CNS side effects at doses close to analgesic doses) has driven research into Cav2.2-targeting analogs with improved therapeutic indices and alternative delivery routes. Ziconotide also serves as a prototypical tool compound for studying N-type calcium channel pharmacology, with applications in mapping Cav2.2 expression patterns in nociceptive pathways, studying the contribution of N-type calcium influx to diverse forms of synaptic plasticity, and developing calcium channel subtype-selective fluorescent indicators using ziconotide scaffold modifications for in vivo neural imaging.",
