    fullDescription: "Pasireotide (SOM230/Signifor) is a synthetic cyclic hexapeptide somatostatin analog developed by Novartis with a uniquely broad affinity profile across somatostatin receptor subtypes. While first-generation somatostatin analogs (octreotide, lanreotide) bind predominantly SSTR2 and, to a lesser extent, SSTR5, pasireotide was engineered with non-natural amino acid substitutions (cyclohexylamine, O-benzyl-tyrosine) that confer high affinity for SSTR1 (IC₅₀ ~9 nM), SSTR2 (IC₅₀ ~1 nM), SSTR3 (IC₅₀ ~2 nM), and SSTR5 (IC₅₀ ~0.2 nM) — a multireceptor binding profile approximately 40-fold superior to octreotide at SSTR1 and SSTR3. This broadened receptor binding profile is particularly important for Cushing's disease research, where corticotroph adenomas expressing SSTR5 but not SSTR2 are unresponsive to octreotide; pasireotide's SSTR5 agonism suppresses ACTH secretion in these tumors where octreotide fails.\n\nIn corticotroph adenoma research, pasireotide at nanomolar concentrations inhibits ACTH secretion in AtT-20 pituitary corticotroph cells and in primary corticotroph adenoma cultures through SSTR5-mediated Gαi signaling that reduces adenylyl cyclase activity, lowers cAMP, and decreases POMC gene expression via reduced CRE-binding protein (CREB) activation. In DEN-induced pituitary tumor rat models and transgenic ACTH-secreting adenoma mouse models, pasireotide treatment reduces tumor ACTH output, normalizes plasma cortisol, reduces tumor volume, and improves metabolic parameters (body weight, glucose tolerance) that are dysregulated by hypercortisolemia. The Phase III PASPORT trial in Cushing's disease demonstrated statistically significant reductions in urinary free cortisol and clinical symptom improvement with pasireotide (Signifor), leading to FDA/EMA approval for Cushing's disease with SSTR2-low tumors.\n\nBeyond Cushing's disease, pasireotide is used in acromegaly research (SSTR2+SSTR5 co-agonism produces superior IGF-1 suppression vs. octreotide alone in some GH-secreting adenomas), neuroendocrine tumor (NET) research, and GH physiology studies. Its multireceptor pharmacology makes pasireotide a valuable tool for studying the contributions of different SSTR subtypes to somatostatin's diverse regulatory functions across pituitary, GI tract (motility, enzyme secretion), immune system, and tumor biology contexts. Research using SSTR-subtype-selective antibodies paired with pasireotide pharmacological analysis in primary pituitary cell cultures has provided insights into the SSTR subtype complement expressed by different pituitary tumor subtypes and their correlation with response to somatostatin analog therapy.",
