    shortDescription: "A 14-amino acid guanylate cyclase-C (GC-C) agonist used in GI research for studying intestinal secretion, motility, and visceral pain signaling mechanisms.",
    fullDescription: "Linaclotide (Linzess/Constella) is a synthetic 14-amino acid peptide agonist of guanylate cyclase-C (GC-C/GUCY2C), a receptor expressed on the luminal surface of intestinal epithelial cells that normally responds to endogenous heat-stable enterotoxin (STa) family peptides including uroguanylin and guanylin. Linaclotide shares the three-disulfide bond scaffold of STa but is structurally optimized for human GC-C agonism with improved receptor affinity (EC₅₀ ~10 nM for cGMP production) and resistance to intraluminal proteolysis. Upon GC-C activation, the receptor's intracellular guanylyl cyclase domain converts GTP to cGMP, which activates protein kinase G-II (PKG-II) and opens the cystic fibrosis transmembrane conductance regulator (CFTR) chloride channel and activates the NHE3 bicarbonate transporter, driving intestinal fluid and bicarbonate secretion. The resulting luminal fluid increase accelerates colonic transit and promotes bowel movement in constipation research models.\n\nIn visceral pain research models, a key mechanistic discovery is that linaclotide's cGMP-mediated effects extend beyond secretion: intraluminal cGMP released by GC-C-expressing colonocytes acts on submucosal nociceptors (afferent pain neurons) via cGMP-gated channels, reducing their firing threshold sensitivity to distension and inflammation. Studies using GC-C-knockout mice demonstrate that linaclotide's pain-reducing effects in colorectal distension protocols are lost in the absence of GC-C, confirming a GC-C-dependent visceral analgesic mechanism distinct from traditional anti-nociceptive pathways. This dual secretagogue + analgesic mechanism makes linaclotide a particularly valuable research tool for studying irritable bowel syndrome with constipation (IBS-C) and chronic idiopathic constipation (CIC) pathophysiology.\n\nLinaclotide (Linzess) is FDA-approved for IBS-C and CIC and serves as a reference GC-C agonist for comparing novel guanylin-family peptide analogs in secretory and pain research. In vitro kinetics studies using T84 and Caco-2 intestinal cell monolayers quantify cGMP production, CFTR-dependent Isc (short-circuit current), and PKG-II phosphorylation as pharmacodynamic readouts. Comparative studies with plecanatide — a GC-C agonist with pH-sensitive activity — have used linaclotide as a reference to understand how luminal pH affects GC-C agonist activity across different intestinal segments, contributing to the design of next-generation GI peptide secretagogues with region-selective action profiles.",
