    fullDescription: "Taltirelin (TA-0910; Ceredist) is a synthetic analog of thyrotropin-releasing hormone (TRH; pGlu-His-Pro-NH₂) in which the pyroglutamate residue of native TRH is replaced by a 5-oxo-imidazolidine ring system, conferring dramatically improved metabolic stability compared to the parent tripeptide. Native TRH is rapidly inactivated in serum by pyroglutamyl aminopeptidase and TRH-degrading ectoenzyme (TDE), with a plasma half-life of only 2-3 minutes, limiting its therapeutic utility. Taltirelin's modified N-terminus resists these enzymes while retaining full agonist activity at both TRH receptor subtypes (TRHR1 and TRHR2), enabling meaningful CNS effects after peripheral administration. Receptor binding assays confirm that taltirelin binds TRHR with IC₅₀ values comparable to native TRH, and functional assays in GH3 rat pituitary cells confirm IP3/DAG/Ca²⁺ mobilization consistent with Gαq/11 signaling downstream of TRHR activation.\n\nIn CNS pharmacology research, taltirelin demonstrates neuroprotective and neuroactivating properties attributed to TRH receptor-mediated enhancement of central monoaminergic neurotransmission. In animal models of spinocerebellar degeneration, taltirelin administration improves motor coordination (rotarod performance), reduces cerebellar atrophy, and upregulates TrkB (BDNF receptor) expression in surviving Purkinje cells and granule neurons. Studies in monoamine depletion models (reserpine-treated rats) show that taltirelin reverses hypothermia, hypolocomotion, and ptosis — effects attributed to TRH receptor-mediated stimulation of dopaminergic, noradrenergic, and serotonergic neurotransmission. Unlike direct monoamine precursors or reuptake inhibitors, taltirelin's effects are mediated through endogenous neurotransmitter system modulation rather than exogenous transmitter loading.\n\nTaltirelin (Ceredist) is approved in Japan for the treatment of spinocerebellar degeneration (SCD) and amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS) to improve motor coordination and voluntary movement. In ALS research models, taltirelin treatment in G93A SOD1 transgenic mice delays disease onset, slows motor neuron loss in the spinal cord anterior horn, and extends survival compared to vehicle controls — effects hypothesized to involve TRH receptor-mediated neuroprotection, anti-excitotoxic activity (TRH antagonizes NMDA and kainate receptor activity), and upregulation of neurotrophic factor production. Its improved pharmacokinetic stability and established CNS activity profile make taltirelin a valuable tool for studying TRH receptor pharmacology, motor neuron disease pathophysiology, and cerebellar circuit preservation in neurodegeneration research.",
