    fullDescription: "Plecanatide (Trulance) is a 16-amino acid synthetic analog of uroguanylin, one of the endogenous activators of guanylate cyclase-C (GC-C/GUCY2C) on intestinal epithelial cells. While linaclotide is derived from the STa (heat-stable enterotoxin) peptide scaffold with pH-independent GC-C agonism, plecanatide was specifically designed to mimic uroguanylin's natural pH-dependent activity: it binds and activates GC-C maximally in the mildly acidic pH environment of the proximal small intestine (pH ~6.8) but has significantly reduced activity in the more neutral or alkaline pH of the large intestine. This pH-sensitive activation is achieved through aspartate and glutamate residues flanking the disulfide-bonded core that alter peptide conformation and receptor interaction as a function of protonation state, replicating the physiological role of uroguanylin as a duodenally-active hormone activated by gastric acid-produced luminal acidification.\n\nIn GC-C signaling research, plecanatide activates the guanylyl cyclase domain of GC-C to produce cGMP, which activates PKG-II and stimulates CFTR-mediated chloride secretion and NHE3-mediated bicarbonate transport into the intestinal lumen, driving the secretory fluid response that accelerates colonic transit. Studies comparing cGMP production in isolated colonocytes versus enterocytes treated with plecanatide versus linaclotide at pH 6.5 and 7.4 have clarified the segment-specific pharmacodynamics, showing plecanatide's relative preference for proximal intestinal activation while maintaining sufficient distal activity to accelerate colonic transit. This pH-dependent pharmacology has also been studied in terms of whether it confers reduced systemic absorption (and therefore reduced secretory diarrhea side effects) compared to pH-independent GC-C agonists.\n\nPlecanatide (Trulance) received FDA approval for chronic idiopathic constipation (CIC) and IBS-C in 2017. In visceral pain research models, plecanatide has been shown to reduce distension-evoked mesenteric nerve firing (visceral afferent sensitization) in mouse colonic preparations, paralleling linaclotide's cGMP-mediated nociceptor modulation through a GC-C-dependent pathway. Research applications include comparative pharmacodynamic studies with linaclotide to determine how activation site and pH-dependence affect secretory and analgesic outcomes in different bowel segments, in vitro transport studies in Caco-2 monolayers measuring paracellular secretion and cGMP efflux, and mechanistic research into the uroguanylin-GC-C-cGMP axis in intestinal epithelial homeostasis, barrier function, and colorectal cancer prevention.",
