    fullDescription: "Daridorexant (Quviviq; ACT-541468) is a potent, selective, and CNS-penetrant dual orexin receptor antagonist (DORA) that blocks both orexin receptor subtypes (OX1R and OX2R) with high affinity (Ki ~1 nM for OX1R, ~2 nM for OX2R). Orexins (hypocretins) are neuropeptides produced by neurons in the lateral hypothalamus that promote wakefulness, arousal, and vigilance by activating orexin receptors throughout the ascending arousal network (locus coeruleus, dorsal raphe, tuberomammillary nucleus, basal forebrain). By competitively blocking both orexin receptor subtypes, daridorexant reduces the orexinergic drive that maintains wakefulness, facilitating sleep onset and maintenance. Unlike benzodiazepines and Z-drugs that broadly potentiate GABAergic inhibition, daridorexant's mechanism specifically targets the wake-promoting orexin system, leaving other sleep-regulating systems (GABA, histamine, adenosine) intact and theoretically producing more physiologically natural sleep.\n\nIn rodent sleep EEG research, daridorexant accelerates sleep onset, increases total sleep time, enhances NREM sleep, and reduces waking after sleep onset (WASO) in dose-dependent fashion without significant suppression of REM sleep at therapeutic doses — a key advantage over older hypnotics. Microdialysis studies measuring monoamine neurotransmitter levels in the waking brain confirm that daridorexant reduces cortical norepinephrine and histamine turnover during the active (dark) phase, consistent with reduced locus coeruleus and tuberomammillary nucleus activation downstream of OX1R/OX2R blockade. The selectivity of daridorexant for orexin receptors versus other sleep-regulatory GPCRs (adenosine A1R, GABAA, H1R) has been validated by radioligand binding panels, confirming >1000-fold selectivity for orexin receptors over 140 other CNS targets.\n\nDaridorexant (Quviviq) received FDA approval in 2022 for adult insomnia treatment and represents the third DORA approved (following suvorexant and lemborexant). In insomnia research, the Phase III EMERGE trials demonstrated statistically significant improvements in sleep onset latency, WASO, total sleep time, and patient-reported next-morning functioning scores versus placebo at 25 mg and 50 mg doses. Research with daridorexant has contributed to understanding the role of orexin signaling in sleep-wake boundary control, and the compound is used in sleep disorder research as a pharmacological tool to acutely suppress orexin neurotransmission and study the contribution of OX1R versus OX2R to different components of arousal behavior — complementing studies using selective OX1R or OX2R antagonists to dissect receptor subtype contributions.",
