    fullDescription: "Terlipressin (glypressin) is a synthetic long-acting prodrug of lysine-vasopressin (LVP) formed by attaching a triglycyl N-terminal extension (Gly-Gly-Gly-) to the N-terminus of the LVP nonapeptide. After administration, terlipressin is enzymatically converted to active LVP in vivo by plasma aminopeptidases that sequentially cleave the three glycyl residues, generating LVP that exerts vasopressin receptor-mediated vasoconstriction. The slow enzymatic release of LVP provides a sustained pharmacological effect over 4–6 hours from a single bolus dose, without the extreme hemodynamic effects associated with direct LVP injection. Terlipressin's primary pharmacodynamic effect is potent splanchnic vasoconstriction mediated by V1a receptor activation on mesenteric and portal arterial smooth muscle, reducing portal venous inflow and portal hypertension.\n\nIn hepatology research, terlipressin is the standard pharmacological model for studying splanchnic vasoconstriction in portal hypertension and hepatorenal syndrome (HRS). In cirrhotic rat models (CCl4-induced or bile duct ligation), terlipressin infusion reduces portal pressure, splenic arterial flow, and variceal pressure as measured by direct portal pressure catheterization. In HRS research, where renal arteriolar vasodilation in the setting of portal hypertension causes profound renal hypoperfusion, terlipressin's V1a-mediated peripheral vasoconstriction redistributes blood flow from the splanchnic bed to the systemic and renal circulation, improving renal perfusion pressure and GFR in rodent and primate HRS models.\n\nTerlipressin (Terlivaz) received FDA approval in 2022 for HRS type 1 (HRS-AKI) in adults — the first approved pharmacological treatment for this life-threatening complication of end-stage liver disease. The Phase III CONFIRM trial demonstrated that terlipressin significantly improved reversal of HRS-AKI (serum creatinine ≤1.5 mg/dL) and transplant-free survival versus placebo. Research applications include investigating the relationship between baseline portal pressure and HRS treatment response, studying V1a receptor expression in cirrhotic vasculature, and exploring terlipressin as a bridge-to-liver transplantation by temporarily improving renal function in patients awaiting organ availability.",
