    fullDescription: "Difelikefalin (CR845; Kapruvia) is a tetrapeptide kappa-opioid receptor (KOR) agonist composed entirely of D-amino acids with an aminobutyric acid modification on the lysine side chain, conferring high KOR selectivity (Ki ~0.4 nM) and near-complete peripheral restriction due to its high hydrophilicity and positive charge that prevents BBB penetration at therapeutic doses. KOR activation by dynorphin neuropeptides in the CNS produces dysphoria and psychotomimetic effects — historically preventing systemic development of KOR agonists. Difelikefalin's peripheral restriction was engineered to achieve KOR-mediated antipruritic and analgesic effects at peripheral sensory neurons (C-fibers) and immune cells without accessing central KOR in mesolimbic circuits. Peripheral KOR is expressed on cutaneous C-fiber afferents, renal tubular cells, and immune cells including T-cells and macrophages.\n\nIn chronic kidney disease-associated pruritus (CKD-aP) research, affecting 40–60% of hemodialysis patients, difelikefalin suppresses itch through peripheral KOR activation on cutaneous C-fiber afferents. In murine pruritus models using intradermal pruritogens (chloroquine, SLIGRL), difelikefalin dose-dependently reduces scratching behavior without producing KOR-associated CNS side effects (sedation, place aversion) in conditioned place aversion assays. Mechanistically, peripheral KOR activation hyperpolarizes C-fiber neurons via Gαi-mediated cAMP inhibition, reduces TRPV1 and TRPA1 channel sensitization, and decreases proinflammatory neuropeptide release downstream of inflammatory pruritogens.\n\nDifelikefalin (Kapruvia) received FDA approval in 2021 for CKD-aP in hemodialysis patients — the first KOR agonist approved for any indication. KALM-1 and KALM-2 Phase III trials demonstrated statistically significant reductions in worst itch intensity NRS scores and improved itch-related quality of life versus placebo. Research applications extend to studying peripherally restricted KOR pharmacology in postoperative pain and visceral pain models, and as a tool for investigating how D-amino acid stereochemistry affects receptor pharmacology, CNS penetrability, and opioid receptor selectivity in comparative peptide pharmacology studies.",
