    fullDescription: "Pegcetacoplan (APL-2; Empaveli/Syfovre) is a PEGylated derivative of the compstatin family cyclic peptide C3 inhibitor, consisting of the Cp40 13-amino acid cyclic peptide conjugated to two 20-kDa PEG chains to extend the plasma half-life from hours (unmodified Cp40) to approximately 7 days, enabling twice-weekly subcutaneous administration. Compstatin binds the C3 and C3b molecule at the convertase docking interface, preventing C3 cleavage by classical (C4b2a), alternative (C3bBb), and lectin pathway C3 convertases — a proximal blockade that simultaneously suppresses all three complement activation pathways. This upstream C3 blockade is mechanistically distinct from C5 inhibitors (eculizumab, ravulizumab, zilucoplan) and prevents generation of both C3b opsonins and C3a anaphylatoxin, in addition to blocking the terminal complement cascade — providing more complete complement suppression.\n\nIn paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria (PNH) research, C3 inhibition is particularly important because 20–30% of PNH patients on C5 inhibitor therapy experience extravascular hemolysis driven by C3b opsonization of GPI-deficient PNH erythrocytes — a pathway not prevented by C5 blockade alone. Pegcetacoplan prevents C3b deposition on PNH red blood cells, eliminating both intravascular (MAC-mediated) and extravascular (C3b/C3d opsonin-mediated splenic phagocytosis) hemolysis pathways simultaneously. In PNH cell model systems, pegcetacoplan at nanomolar concentrations prevents C3b surface deposition on GPI-deficient erythrocytes as quantified by flow cytometry using anti-C3b antibody staining after serum activation.\n\nIn geographic atrophy (dry AMD) research, complement activation in the retinal pigment epithelium and Bruch's membrane has been implicated in photoreceptor atrophy progression. The DERBY and OAKS Phase III trials demonstrated that monthly or bimonthly intravitreal pegcetacoplan injections significantly slowed geographic atrophy lesion growth rate versus sham injection, confirming complement-mediated RPE damage as a pharmacologically addressable target. Pegcetacoplan (Syfovre) received FDA approval for geographic atrophy secondary to AMD in 2023 — the first approved treatment for this condition — with the PNH indication (Empaveli) using subcutaneous administration, providing a unique model for comparing local versus systemic complement inhibition via different delivery routes.",
