    fullDescription: "Angiotensin II (Ang II) is the principal bioactive octapeptide effector of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS), generated by angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) cleavage of the C-terminal His-Leu dipeptide from angiotensin I, which is itself produced by renin cleavage of angiotensinogen. Ang II binds both AT1R (Gαq/11-coupled GPCR mediating vasoconstriction, aldosterone secretion, and sodium retention) and AT2R (Gαi-coupled, opposing AT1R, mediating vasodilation, natriuresis, and anti-proliferative effects), with the balance of AT1R and AT2R activity determining net cardiovascular and renal outcomes. In clinical hypertension research, Ang II is administered by continuous IV infusion to create pharmacological hypertension models for studying RAAS dysregulation, AT1R-mediated inflammatory signaling, and AT1R blockade by ARB drugs (losartan, valsartan) in vascular biology.\n\nIn cardiovascular research, Ang II at supraphysiological concentrations via osmotic minipump generates consistent hypertension (50–70 mmHg blood pressure elevation), cardiac fibrosis, aortic stiffness, and renal inflammation in mice and rats over 2–4 weeks — providing standardized models for testing ACEi and ARB compounds. AT1R activation of NADPH oxidase (NOX2/4) generates superoxide and hydrogen peroxide, creating an oxidative stress environment in vascular smooth muscle and endothelial cells that activates NF-κB and AP-1 inflammatory pathways — a mechanism being studied for its contribution to hypertensive vascular injury and atherosclerosis progression.\n\nAngiotensin II (Giapreza) is FDA-approved as a vasopressor for adults with refractory distributive shock, the first new natural vasopressor class approved in decades. Basic RAAS research applications of synthetic Ang II include AT1R and AT2R signaling cross-talk studies, receptor internalization kinetics, beta-arrestin-biased AT1R signaling (versus Gαq/11 coupling), and mapping of Ang II metabolite pharmacology — including Ang III, Ang IV, and Ang-(1-7) — which activate distinct receptor subtypes (IRAP, Mas receptor) and produce effects opposing Ang II's vasoconstriction, highlighting RAAS as an intricately balanced multi-peptide regulatory network.",
