    fullDescription: "Carbetocin (Duratocin; Pabal) is a synthetic long-acting analog of oxytocin designed to reduce postpartum hemorrhage (PPH) at cesarean delivery. Its molecular structure differs from natural oxytocin in three key ways: replacement of the N-terminal hemicystine ring with a 1-butanoic acid moiety (removing the disulfide and creating a monocyclic peptide), substitution of tyrosine-2 with O-methyltyrosine (conferring OTR selectivity and metabolic stability), and replacement of cysteine-1 with a carbon chain forming a carba bridge rather than the disulfide — providing 4–10 times longer duration of action than oxytocin (4–7 hours versus 6–12 minutes). Carbetocin binds OTR with high selectivity over vasopressin V1a/V2 receptors (>100-fold), coupling to Gαq/11 signaling that elevates intracellular calcium in uterine smooth muscle and stimulates myometrial contraction for sustained uterine tone after delivery.\n\nIn uterine contractility research, carbetocin in isolated human myometrial strip preparations produces sustained tetanic contractions with significantly prolonged duration compared to oxytocin — typically 45–60 minutes versus <10 minutes. Studies comparing OTR internalization rates after carbetocin versus oxytocin stimulation in CHO-OTR cells show carbetocin produces slower receptor internalization, maintaining surface receptor density longer and contributing to the prolonged contractile effect. In pregnant rat uterine preparations, carbetocin at 0.1–1 μM concentrations produced sustained regular contractions — the pharmacodynamic pattern sought for postpartum hemorrhage prevention.\n\nCarbetocin has been approved in more than 90 countries for prevention of PPH in cesarean delivery, with a thermostable formulation approved by WHO Prequalification for resource-limited settings. The WOMAN-2 trial demonstrated that carbetocin significantly reduced PPH incidence versus oxytocin in vaginal delivery settings in low- and middle-income countries. Research applications include studying OTR signaling in CNS contexts (OTR in hypothalamus and limbic system mediating social bonding and stress response), investigating carbetocin's potential for autism spectrum disorder (targeting OTR-mediated prosocial behavior), and characterizing the structural requirements for OTR versus vasopressin receptor selectivity across the oxytocin/vasopressin peptide family.",
