    fullDescription: "Atosiban (Tractocile) is a competitive antagonist of both the oxytocin receptor (OTR) and vasopressin V1a receptor (V1aR), structurally derived from oxytocin with key modifications that convert agonist activity to antagonism: removal of the N-terminal amino group (1-deamino), replacement of Tyr² with D-Tyr(ethyl)² (D-stereochemistry and ethyl ether on tyrosine ring), substitution of Ile⁴ with threonine, and replacement of Leu⁸ with ornithine. These modifications disrupt the structural requirements for OTR activation while maintaining receptor binding affinity (Ki ~1–10 nM at OTR), converting the molecule from a potent agonist to a competitive antagonist that blocks endogenous oxytocin and vasopressin binding without triggering Gαq-IP3-calcium signaling in uterine myocytes. The dual OTR/V1aR antagonism is relevant to preterm labor tocolysis because vasopressin also contributes to uterine contractility through V1aR at term and in preterm labor.\n\nIn uterine contractility research, atosiban in isolated human myometrial strips and in pregnant uterus models dose-dependently relaxes spontaneous and oxytocin-induced contractions, reduces uterine pressure, and prolongs inter-contraction intervals at concentrations of 10–100 nM. Mechanistic studies confirm that atosiban prevents oxytocin-induced IP3-mediated Ca²⁺ release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum and extracellular Ca²⁺ influx through VGCC, producing tocolytic effects by maintaining low myocyte Ca²⁺ and reducing myosin light chain kinase (MLCK) activity. Binding studies comparing atosiban versus OTR antagonists L-368,899 and retosiban confirm that atosiban occupies the orthosteric OTR binding site, competitively displacing both oxytocin and vasopressin.\n\nAtosiban (Tractocile) is approved in Europe for delaying premature birth in women with preterm labor between 24 and 33 weeks of gestation, where it reduces uterine contractility and allows time for corticosteroid administration to accelerate fetal lung maturity. Research applications include studying the physiological roles of oxytocin and vasopressin in normal term labor initiation, investigating OTR versus V1aR contributions to different components of uterine contractility across pregnancy, and developing more selective OTR antagonists for tocolysis. Atosiban is also used in reproductive research to study embryo implantation, where uterine receptivity and myometrial quiescence are coordinated by oxytocin and atosiban provides a pharmacological tool for studying OTR's role in endometrial-myometrial crosstalk during embryo transfer.",
