    fullDescription: "Mifamurtide (L-MTP-PE; Mepact) is a synthetic liposomal formulation of muramyl dipeptide (MDP; N-acetylmuramyl-L-alanyl-D-isoglutamine) — the minimal immunostimulatory structural unit of bacterial peptidoglycan — chemically conjugated via a lysine spacer to a phospholipid fatty acid tail (C18:1) and encapsulated in multilamellar DPPC/DPPG liposomes. The liposomal formulation enables IV delivery and targets monocytes and macrophages via phagocytic uptake, where the endolysosomal environment releases the MDP conjugate to engage intracellular NOD2 (CARD15) receptors. NOD2 recognizes MDP as a bacterial danger signal, activating the RIP2 kinase-NF-κB signaling pathway that drives macrophage and monocyte tumoricidal activation — increasing production of TNF-α, IL-1β, IL-12, and nitric oxide, and enhancing phagocytic and cytotoxic activities against tumor cells.\n\nIn osteosarcoma research, the rationale for macrophage activation as an anti-tumor strategy is based on the observation that activated macrophages in the tumor microenvironment exhibit enhanced tumoricidal activity via contact-dependent mechanisms and cytokine secretion. In rodent pulmonary metastasis models using OS-3 and LM8 osteosarcoma cell lines, mifamurtide encapsulated in multilamellar liposomes significantly reduces lung metastasis formation compared to free MDP (which is not macrophage-targeted) or empty liposomes — confirming that liposomal delivery to alveolar macrophages is essential for anti-metastatic efficacy. Ex vivo cytotoxicity assays confirm mifamurtide-treated macrophages show enhanced tumoricidal activity against osteosarcoma targets with increased MHC II, CD80, CD86, and CD40 surface expression consistent with an M1 pro-inflammatory activated macrophage phenotype.\n\nMifamurtide (Mepact) received EMA approval for high-grade, resectable, non-metastatic osteosarcoma as adjuvant to standard chemotherapy, based on retrospective analysis of a Phase III trial demonstrating improved 6-year overall survival (78% vs 70%) when added to MAP chemotherapy (methotrexate, adriamycin, cisplatin). Research applications include studying innate immune activation mechanisms, macrophage polarization dynamics under MDP stimulation, the NOD2 signaling pathway in cancer immunosurveillance, and the design of liposomal innate immune agonist delivery systems for cancer immunotherapy applications. Its phospholipid-MDP conjugate structure also makes it a research tool for studying liposome-macrophage endosomal release pharmacokinetics and the relationship between intracellular NOD2 activation and M1/M2 macrophage polarization in the tumor microenvironment.",
