    fullDescription: "Carfilzomib (Kyprolis; PR-171) is a tetrapeptide epoxyketone proteasome inhibitor that irreversibly and covalently modifies the N-terminal threonine of the β5 (PSMB5) chymotryptic-like (CT-L) and β5i (PSMB8) immunoproteasome subunit active sites through a selective epoxyketone pharmacophore. Upon carfilzomib binding, the α,β-epoxyketone reacts with the catalytic N-terminal threonine of β5/β5i through sequential addition reactions forming a morpholine ring adduct — an irreversible covalent modification distinct from bortezomib's reversible boronic acid mechanism. This irreversible β5 inhibition produces prolonged proteasomal blockade persisting until new proteasome subunits are synthesized, providing more sustained UPS inhibition per dose. Carfilzomib shows high selectivity for β5 over β2 (tryptic-like) and β1 (caspase-like) subunits at therapeutic concentrations, minimizing off-target protease inhibition.\n\nIn multiple myeloma (MM) research, proteasome inhibition by carfilzomib triggers unresolved endoplasmic reticulum (ER) stress through accumulation of misfolded immunoglobulin proteins that MM cells produce at extremely high rates. Western blot analysis in carfilzomib-treated MM cell lines (RPMI-8226, U266, OPM-2) shows dose-dependent accumulation of polyubiquitinated proteins, upregulation of CHOP, BiP/GRP78, and IRE1α — hallmarks of the unfolded protein response (UPR) and ER stress — followed by JNK activation, cytochrome c release, and caspase-8/9/3 activation indicative of intrinsic and extrinsic apoptotic pathway engagement. Notably, carfilzomib retains activity in bortezomib-resistant MM cell lines bearing β5 mutations that prevent bortezomib boronic acid binding.\n\nCarfilzomib (Kyprolis) received FDA approval for relapsed/refractory MM, with Phase III trials (ASPIRE, ENDEAVOR) demonstrating significant progression-free survival improvements over comparator regimens. Research with carfilzomib has advanced understanding of proteasome biology, MM cell vulnerability to UPS dysfunction, and the structural basis of epoxyketone versus boronic acid selectivity in proteasome inhibitor design. The compound is widely used as a tool to study protein degradation pathways, NF-κB signaling (which is regulated by proteasomal IκB degradation), antigen presentation (which requires proteasomal MHC class I antigen processing), and immunoproteasome biology in dendritic cells and macrophages.",
