    fullDescription: "Bremelanotide (PT-141; Vyleesi) is a cyclic heptapeptide melanocortin receptor agonist derived from the Melanotan II scaffold, sharing the core Ac-cyclo[Nle⁴,D-Phe⁷]-alpha-MSH(4-10) pharmacophore sequence that provides high affinity for MC3R and MC4R (nanomolar Ki values), with reduced activity at MC1R compared to full-length alpha-MSH. MC4R is highly expressed in hypothalamic paraventricular nucleus (PVN) neurons and limbic system circuits that regulate sexual arousal and motivation. MC4R activation drives sexual arousal responses through stimulation of oxytocin release from PVN neurons and activation of dopaminergic reward pathways in the mesolimbic system — providing a neuroendocrine mechanism for bremelanotide's pro-sexual pharmacology independent of sex steroid hormones or peripheral vascular effects.\n\nIn rodent sexual behavior research, bremelanotide SC administration increases mounting behavior, intromission frequency, and lordosis responses in male and female rats respectively, demonstrating that MC4R agonism centrally facilitates sexual behavior in both sexes. The central mechanism is confirmed by intracerebroventricular administration directly into the PVN, which produces sexual arousal at much lower doses than peripheral administration, and by attenuation with oxytocin receptor antagonists — indicating PVN oxytocin release downstream of MC4R activation is a key mechanistic step. Dopamine D1/D2 receptor antagonists also partially attenuate bremelanotide's pro-sexual effects in rodent models, implicating mesolimbic dopaminergic pathway co-activation.\n\nBremelanotide (Vyleesi) received FDA approval in 2019 for hypoactive sexual desire disorder (HSDD) in premenopausal women — the only approved SC peptide for female sexual dysfunction. The RECONNECT Phase III trials demonstrated statistically significant improvements in desire and distress scores versus placebo with on-demand SC bremelanotide use. Research with bremelanotide provides pharmacological insights into MC3R versus MC4R contribution to pro-sexual versus other melanocortin-mediated effects (energy balance, feeding behavior, anxiety), and the compound is used as a reference in MC4R agonist research for studying hypothalamic circuits integrating nutritional status, stress, and reproductive behavior.",
