    fullDescription: "Aviptadil is synthetic vasoactive intestinal peptide (VIP), a 28-amino acid neuropeptide structurally related to secretin, glucagon, and PACAP. VIP acts on two closely related class B GPCRs: VPAC1 (expressed on lymphocytes, gut epithelium, lung, hypothalamus) and VPAC2 (expressed on smooth muscle, lung, suprachiasmatic nucleus, pancreas), both coupled to Gαs-adenylyl cyclase-cAMP-PKA signaling, producing vasodilation, bronchial smooth muscle relaxation, increased mucus secretion, and anti-inflammatory cytokine shifts (increased IL-10, reduced TNF-α and IL-6). In the lung, VIP is produced by non-adrenergic non-cholinergic (NANC) nerves and type II alveolar epithelial cells (ATII cells), where it serves as a local bronchoprotective and cytoprotective neuropeptide. VPAC1 and VPAC2 are highly expressed on ATII cells — the primary gas-exchange and surfactant-producing cells of the alveolus.\n\nIn ARDS research, aviptadil has been studied for its potential to protect ATII cells from inflammatory injury through VPAC-mediated pathways. In vitro, aviptadil protects ATII cells from LPS-induced apoptosis (reducing caspase-3/7 activation), cytokine-induced ROS generation, and surfactant protein B downregulation. In LPS-induced acute lung injury animal models, IV or inhaled aviptadil reduces neutrophil alveolar infiltration (BAL differential cell count), inflammatory cytokine levels in BAL fluid, and histological injury scores. These ARDS-relevant protective effects led to clinical investigation of aviptadil (RLF-100) for COVID-19 ARDS, where early Phase I/II trials in critically ill patients suggested improved respiratory outcomes.\n\nAviptadil is used in pulmonary hypertension research as a vasodilator and anti-remodeling agent, where cAMP-mediated pulmonary arterial smooth muscle relaxation and anti-proliferative effects on PASMCs are studied in monocrotaline-induced and hypoxia-induced PH models. In neuroendocrinology research, VIP's role as a master circadian regulator in the suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN) — where VIP-expressing neurons synchronize peripheral circadian clocks via VPAC2-mediated intercellular signaling — has been studied using aviptadil to rescue circadian arrhythmicity in SCN slice preparations with VIP neuron-specific ablation. Its anti-inflammatory neuropeptide role is also investigated in models of sepsis, rheumatoid arthritis, and inflammatory bowel disease.",
