    fullDescription: "Afamelanotide (Scenesse; [Nle⁴,D-Phe⁷]-alpha-MSH; NDP-alpha-MSH) is a synthetic linear 13-amino acid analog of alpha-melanocyte-stimulating hormone incorporating two key substitutions: norleucine (Nle) at position 4 replacing methionine (susceptible to oxidation) and D-phenylalanine (D-Phe) at position 7 replacing native L-Phe. The D-Phe⁷ substitution dramatically increases MC1R binding affinity (>50-fold versus native alpha-MSH) and resistance to aminopeptidase degradation, while the Nle⁴ substitution improves metabolic stability by replacing the Met residue vulnerable to oxidative inactivation. Together these modifications make afamelanotide a potent, metabolically stable MC1R superagonist with plasma half-life of approximately 6–10 hours, enabling subcutaneous polylactic acid implant formulations releasing drug over 60 days for sustained melanogenesis stimulation in photodermatology research.\n\nIn melanocyte biology research, afamelanotide at nanomolar concentrations activates MC1R-Gαs-adenylyl cyclase-cAMP-PKA signaling, leading to CREB phosphorylation, MITF upregulation, and transcriptional activation of melanogenic enzyme genes: tyrosinase (TYR), TYRP1, and DCT/TYRP2. PKA-mediated phosphorylation of MITF-Ser298 enhances transcriptional activity and promotes eumelanin (brown/black pigment) synthesis over pheomelanin (red/yellow), shifting melanin type toward the more photoprotective eumelanin form. Studies in primary human melanocyte cultures confirm that afamelanotide produces sustained eumelanin synthesis lasting 7–14 days after a single treatment, consistent with MC1R-driven transcriptional reprogramming rather than acute enzyme activation.\n\nAfamelanotide (Scenesse) received FDA and EMA approval for prevention of phototoxicity in adults with erythropoietic protoporphyria (EPP) — a rare genetic disorder causing extreme photosensitivity due to ferrochelatase deficiency and protoporphyrin IX accumulation. SC implants releasing afamelanotide over 60 days promote constitutive epidermal eumelanin formation providing a natural melanin-based light filter, increasing sun exposure tolerance. Research applications include studying the relationship between MC1R polymorphisms and melanoma risk, investigating MC1R-mediated DNA repair mechanisms in keratinocytes (nucleotide excision repair upregulation through p53 stabilization), and studying photoprotection biology in models expressing constitutive MC1R versus non-functional MC1R variants.",
