    fullDescription: "Enfuvirtide (T-20; Fuzeon) is a 36-amino acid synthetic peptide derived from the C-terminal heptad repeat (HR2/C34 region) of HIV-1 gp41, a transmembrane envelope glycoprotein that mediates membrane fusion between HIV-1 and target CD4+ T-cells. The HIV-1 fusion process requires gp41 to undergo a dramatic conformational change: after CD4 and coreceptor binding by gp120, gp41 inserts its N-terminal fusion peptide into the host cell membrane and folds back upon itself to align the N-heptad repeat (HR1) and C-heptad repeat (HR2) domains into a thermostable six-helix bundle (6-HB) coiled-coil structure that forces viral and host cell membranes together, driving lipid bilayer merger. Enfuvirtide mimics the HR2 domain and competitively binds the exposed HR1 coiled-coil groove during the pre-6-HB intermediate state, preventing HR1-HR2 interaction and six-helix bundle formation — blocking membrane fusion in a mechanism that acts at the pre-fusion intermediate.\n\nIn HIV-1 entry research, enfuvirtide is used as a mechanistic probe to define the kinetics and structural intermediates of gp41-mediated membrane fusion. Single-molecule FRET studies using fluorescently labeled gp41-expressing cells and target membranes confirm that enfuvirtide traps the gp41 pre-hairpin intermediate — a key mechanistic validation of its inhibition mechanism. Resistance studies in vitro and in clinical isolates have identified that enfuvirtide resistance mutations map primarily to the HR1 region (positions 36-45 of gp41), directly confirming the HR1 interaction interface through evolutionary pressure mapping. Second-generation HIV fusion inhibitors (albuvirtide, sifuvirtide) have been compared with enfuvirtide in HR1 binding assays, six-helix bundle inhibition kinetics, and resistance mutation susceptibility profiles.\n\nEnfuvirtide (Fuzeon) received FDA approval in 2003 as the first HIV fusion inhibitor, administered as twice-daily SC injections in treatment-experienced HIV-1 patients. The TORO-1 and TORO-2 Phase III trials demonstrated significant reductions in HIV-1 RNA versus optimized background therapy in multi-drug resistant HIV-1 patients. Research with enfuvirtide has provided fundamental insights into class-specific HIV-1 entry inhibition, the pre-fusion gp41 intermediate as a drug target, the structural biology of coiled-coil inhibitors, and resistance monitoring assay development. Its well-characterized binding mechanism has also served as a paradigm for designing fusion inhibitors targeting other enveloped viruses (SARS-CoV-2, RSV, Ebola) where similar HR1-HR2 coiled-coil fusion mechanisms operate.",
